self.registry={"en/00-vision.html":{"title":"Vision — what Aletheia is, and the failures it exists to close","content":"The full name is Aletheia; the shortened form theia names the\nCLI, the per-project.theia/ home, the user config, and the repos.\n1. The compounding failure\nAgentic coding today compounds four losses that no single tool fixes: agents\nre-explore the repository on every run and still miss the global picture\n(runtime exploration costs 4–130× the tokens of a precomputed map and produced\n39 defects across 19 industrial tasks where an indexed approach produced zero —\nAOCI), their working context grows until it poisons itself (append-only\nruntimes decay from 0.90 to 0.74 accuracy as horizons stretch, and drop to\n0.53 under realistic telemetry noise — SKILL.state), what they learned\nyesterday is gone tomorrow (no memory survives the session, so every\narchitectural rationale is re-derived at full price), and nothing they claim\nis checkable (a \"done\" without an executed artifact, a number without a\nreceipt, a log an agent can silently rewrite). Teams answer with process —\nchecklists, human gates, playbooks — which is necessary and insufficient: a\nconvention whose only guardian is a document rots (nine production codebases,\nshared-structure analysis).\nAletheia's thesis: these are one platform problem. A stable, incrementally\nmaintained model of the codebase; a bounded, replayable execution state;\na temporal memory that makes yesterday queryable; and an evaluation spine that turns every claim into an artifact — built as one local-first system\naround the harnesses engineers already run (Claude Code, Codex), not as a\nreplacement for them.\n2. The product surface\ntheia — one busybox-style binary/CLI. Subcommands are deployment\nunits: theia index (codebase model), theia run (orchestrated\nexecution), theia mem (memory ops), theia eval (benchmarks & receipts), theia serve (MCP server + metrics + the freshness reconciler), theia replay (deterministic re-execution), theia jail (the\nbwrap-backed sandbox primitive — 10-integrations.md §6 ), plus the run-surface\nverbs ( plan, show, fork, compare, probe, ask, msg, diagnose, audit, prune ) and the distribution verbs\n( 16-release.md ).\n.theia/ — per-project state: user-versioned prompts and YAML config,\nhash-addressed indexes, event logs, memory stores. Greppable by design,\nFUSE-mountable later ( 10-integrations.md §4 ).\nIntegrations — Aletheia is the memory, map, and audit layer under Claude Code and Codex (session hooks, MCP tools, session resume), and the\norchestrator over them (the gateway drives both as harnesses).\n3. Who it serves\nThe plus8soft guidebooks (refs, docs 2–3) define the buyer's shape precisely:\na regulated engineering organization that wants agent leverage with human\ngates, data-class discipline, tamper-evident audit, and KPI-provable pilots.\nTheir L1 is a playbook; their L2 — portal, workflow, policy engine, skills\ncatalog, central audit — is a slide. Aletheia is that L2 built as a real\nsystem, keeping their process shape (tracks, gates, roles, data classes,\nKPIs — mechanized in 11-governance.md ) and adding what\na playbook cannot state: determinism, freshness, cost-per-solved-unit, and\naudit completeness as measured properties (A19).\n4. What \"outperform in every possible metric\" means, concretely Metric class Their instrument Aletheia's instrument\nLead time / rework / defects\nweekly report, hand-filled\neval subsystem: ability-axis scoring on standing benchmarks, receipts per mechanism ( 08-evaluation.md )\nAudit\nagent_run_id in Jira + WORM policy\nHLC-ordered, hash-chained event log; OTel traces; replay equality ( 09-observability.md )\nSafety\nMCP least-privilege matrix in prose\npolicy values consumed by the kernel; data-class gate at the one seam all calls cross ( 04-model-gateway.md )\nQuality gates\nCI contract + Stop & Fix convention\nphase FSM with verified transitions; verifier verdicts from executed artifacts (A2, A12)\nCost\ncost cap \"по политике\"\nper-run locked USD from a price table, budgets as policy, cost-vs-accuracy on the eval frontier (A9)\nKnowledge\nConfluence spec pages\ncodebase intent index + temporal memory, both incrementally fresh and snapshot-queryable\n5. Non-goals (v1)\nNot a model host. Aletheia drives subscription harnesses and APIs; it\ndoes not serve weights.\nNot a cloud SaaS. Local-first, single-operator; the storage interfaces\nare sized for a later Postgres-backed multi-user deployment in which local\nstores become cooperative in-progress state (A15), but v1 ships local.\nNot an IDE. Surfaces are CLI, daemon APIs, MCP, and the harnesses'\nown UIs.\nContainer execution is a seam, not a v1 feature. The bwrap jail\nships (host read-only, granted-RW, probed by doctor); docker-wrapped\nexternal tools ride its spawn-wrapper seam later, and the jail\nresiduals (full subreaper sweep, per-harness RW registry) stay named\ndeferred work ( 13-roadmap.md ).\n6. Naming\nOne name per mechanism, fixed here and used verbatim everywhere: the event log (per-concern SoT), generations (immutable derived-state\nbuilds), the head (served generation pointer), Σ (a run's typed\nexecution state), the plan DAG, the ready frontier, verdicts (typed role outcomes), the gateway (model seam), harness (external\nagent CLI/SDK), terryid (typed HLC id), the intent index and AST\nfacts (codebase model tiers), canonical facts and scoped trees (memory), the ledger (methods-tried record), receipts (benchmark\nevidence), policy values (typed governance knobs).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/index.html":{"title":"Theia platform reference","content":"This is the reference documentation of the Theia platform: the engineering\ndocuments of dokimelabs/theia-dev, published as they are written. They\ndescribe how the platform works and why — architecture, storage, memory,\norchestration, evaluation, observability, governance, the release pipeline.\nFor installing and using the theia CLI, read the user guide first. The\nreference set is for readers who want the design behind the tool.\nWhat is published\nThe numbered document set ( 00 – 17 ) and the guided walkthrough\n( demo.md ). Internal working material is not published: session notes,\nthe evaluation ledger and receipts, and design documents still under\nreview. Where a page mentions such a document, its file name appears as\nplain text without a link.\nSections Section What is inside\nPlatform\nVision, architecture, the guided walkthrough\nCore systems\nIdentity, storage, model gateway, codebase model, orchestration, memory\nQuality and operations\nEvaluation, observability, governance, invariants\nSurfaces and configuration\nIntegrations, TUI and CLI, configuration\nProject and distribution\nRoadmap, release and distribution, collaboration","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/01-architecture.html":{"title":"Architecture — layers, seams, deployment units","content":"The base shape. Subsystem contracts live in their own docs; this one holds\nthe layer map, the five seams and their vocabularies, the derived-state\nplane, and the monorepo layout. Decisions cited A1–A24\n( notes/01-crossanalysis.md ); principles\nP1–P12 and decision rules D1–D8 per the shared-structure doctrine, adopted\nwholesale (A1–A3).\n1. The layer map\nFive layers, five seams. Only the seams are rigid (P1, P3); interiors are\nfree. Every box below is a package or a set of packages\n( §5 ).\nLayer rules (each with its interpreter, P2 — enforcement table in 12-invariants.md ):\nLayer May import Must not Owns\nSurfaces\nengines, schema\nstores, gateway, harness SDKs\nparsing, session extraction, wire mapping; no business logic (claudexor INV-002: a surface never invents state the engine does not own)\nEngines\ndomain, adapters, config\nother engines' internals, surface types\norchestration order, transaction boundary, authz decision\nDomain\ndomain, schema\nany I/O, Date.now() (clock injected), env\ndecisions, invariants, FSM transition tables, merge/interval algebra\nAdapters\nSDKs/drivers, config\nengines, domain decisions\nI/O, driver-level retries, wire⇄row mapping\n2. The five seams and their vocabularies (P3)\nEach seam has ONE result vocabulary, defined in packages/schema, used by\nevery implementation on that seam. Off-seam code returns plain values and\nthrows (P8, D3).\nWire ⇄ engine. Zod-strict schema per operation; wire-as-DTO inside a\nslice (D1). User-visible failure = typed {alias, params}; unexpected =\nthrow, mapped once per surface.\nEngine ⇄ domain. No ceremony: direct calls, exhaustive switches over\nclosed unions.\nStore contract ( 03-storage.md ). Repos are the only\nSQL homes; withTx(closure) owns transactions at the engine level; every\nmulti-writer row carries OCC (generation head or version token);\nidempotency keys are deterministic business-key digests.\nGateway contract ( 04-model-gateway.md ). One\ntagged result across every provider and harness: ok | retryable | fatal | needs_user | truncated | refused, plus\nnormalized usage and uncertainty. Adapters classify; the caller owns\ntime (retry/backoff/fallback). Thrown inside an adapter = transient.\nTool contract ( 10-integrations.md ). Typed\ntool defs with validators; results are evidence objects (exit codes,\nartifact hashes), never prose (A12); registry-dispatched (P10).\n3. The derived-state plane (A1, P7)\nEverything derived — the intent index, AST facts, embeddings, BM25/FAISS\nindexes, memory tree summaries, causal graphs, Σ projections, rendered\nreports — obeys one contract:\nBuilders write generation N+1 aside; SoT rows and the served\ngeneration are never mutated in place.\nPublish = one OCC head compare-and-set. Zero rows updated ⇒ a concurrent\nbuild won; the loser abandons. A crashed build leaves garbage (GC'd),\nnever corruption.\nReaders resolve through the head and accept only monotonically newer\ngenerations; rollback is a pointer flip.\nLatency path: change-triggered incremental builds (content-hash diff).\nConvergence path: level-triggered reconcilers recompute desired state and\nwrite on diff. Both always ship together (D4).\nEvery generation is stamped with the builder version and its input hashes\n— trustworthy reset, rollback, and cache invalidation fall out.\nThis single plane is what MemForest, MV-PBT, the Fidant edge build, and\nplutonium/callisto each built a private copy of; Aletheia builds it once and\nthreads every subsystem through it.\n4. Event sourcing — the run/audit spine\nEach concern keeps an event log as its SoT (redel's replayable JSONL,\noverseer's tasks.events.jsonl, hardened):\nEvents are terryid-stamped (HLC — 02-identity.md ), so\none global ORDER BY id is causally consistent and \"state at t\" is a query\n(a-posteriori snapshots, hlc-snap).\nEvery nondeterministic input — model replies, tool outputs, clock reads,\nrandom draws — is an event; replay = re-reading the log with the\ngateway in replay mode (A22).\nLogs are hash-chained (each event carries the previous event's digest) —\ntamper-evident by construction, satisfying the competitor's WORM/audit\nrequirement without special storage ( 09-observability.md §4 ).\nMutable views (ticket tables, Σ, dashboards) are derived state over the log\n(§3), rebuilt by replay.\n5. Monorepo layout\nTypeScript, pnpm workspace, strict TS, zx for all shell/subprocess work\n(A20). Deployment units live under apps/ and stay thin; libraries under packages/. Shape (vocabulary, not mandate — P9; packages are born on first\nreal consumer, never speculatively):\ntheia/ apps/ theia/ busybox CLI + TUI + daemon verbs (serve = MCP server, /metrics, the reconciler loop); the one binary packages/ schema/ ALL shared contracts: zod schemas, closed unions, verdicts, event shapes, policy value types (INV-020: schema changes first; staged-field rule enforced) terryid/ id + HLC (zero-dep leaf; mirrors proofs/) event-log/ append, hash-chain, replay, snapshot queries store-sqlite/ repos, migrations, withTx, generation/head helpers index-faiss/ vector index adapter (generation-addressed) index-bm25/ lexical index adapter (tantivy-backed; optional pre-analyzed terms) text-analysis/ the lexical analyzer (Unicode segmentation + multi-language Snowball) gateway/ model gateway engine + seam vocabulary + mask gate harness-codex/ adapter: codex CLI (subscription, exec/resume) harness-claude/ adapter: Claude Code CLI (print-mode stream, resume) harness-fake/ adapter: recorded/synthesized — the simulator seam embeddings-local/ ONNX embedder (closed model registry, self-fetching model store) — the default embeddings-openai/ text-embedding-3-small adapter (dims recorded) treesitter/ language grammars, AST facts (deterministic) codebase-model/ intent index builders + retrieval over the map kernel/ coordinator, phase FSM, pools, Σ, plan DAG, prompts memory/ canonical facts, scoped trees, retrieval lanes eval/ index-bench, calibration, swebl, demo environment mcp-server/ `theia serve` tool handlers over the same engines otel/ tracing/metrics facade (PII denylist at boundary) config/ YAML loader: apiVersion/kind manifests, validation proofs/ Coq: hlc.v, state_merge.v (+ CI check) docs/ this set\nVocabulary notes (P9 — packages are born on a real consumer, never\nspeculatively): policy value types live in schema (a policy package\ngraduates when a second consumer class appears); tool execution lives in\nthe kernel's runner + the MCP belt ( tools-* packages graduate with the\ncontainer provider); the daemon surface is theia serve inside the one\nbinary (a separate app graduates with the daemon channel). Dependency law: schema and terryid import nothing workspace-local; adapters never\nimport engines; harness-* are translational only (claudexor INV-013 — no\norchestration, budgets, or review policy inside an adapter); surfaces\nimport engines only. apps/* are composition roots that wire dependencies\nvisibly (D8 — no DI container).\n6. Configuration\nYAML manifests, k8s-shaped ( apiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1, closed kind set), living in.theia/config/ under the user's VCS\n( 10-integrations.md §4 ). Placement follows the\nconfig-placement decision tree: secrets/deploy identity → env (validated at\nboot, fail-fast); tunable knobs → typed config values with env override and\nloud fallback; structural enums → code constants; per-project policy → YAML\nmanifests. Every knob names a consumer or fails CI (staged-field rule, A3);\nprompt-side enum lists interpolate from the code enums (.join('|') ), never\nduplicate.\n7. What deliberately stays fat\nThe kernel's coordinator and the memory engine's retrieval core may be large\ncohesive files (D6): their leaves are thin, their invariants are documented\ninline, and the weekly change (add a role / lane / probe) touches a registry\nline, not the core. The review question is never \"how long?\" but \"does the\nweekly change touch this file?\".","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/02-identity.html":{"title":"Identity — terryid: typed, causally ordered IDs","content":"1. The failure this closes\nDistributed and multi-process systems that stamp records with wall time alone\ncannot order causally related events (NTP skew reorders them, POSIX time even\nruns backwards across leap-second smearing), systems that use Lamport clocks\nlose the wall-time meaning applications need, and systems that use random\nUUIDs get neither — plus indexes that fragment on insert. Aletheia adds a\nfourth requirement none of those satisfy: an ID must say what it names (a run vs a fact vs an event) so a wrong-kind reference is unconstructible\n(P10). terryid answers all four at once.\n2. The format\n<ppp> <22 chars base32, lowercase> type prefix + body = base32( l ‖ c ‖ node ‖ random )\nppp — a 3-character type prefix from a closed registry in packages/schema (e.g. run, evt, fct, chk, gen, pln, vrd, art ). The registry is the interpreter: constructing or parsing an ID\nchecks the prefix against the union, so a fct -typed field cannot hold a\nrun id (zp0 terryid's prefix discipline + typed wrapper, sharpened by the\nclosed union).\nbody — 110 bits, base32 (RFC 4648 lowercase, no padding):\nl: 48 bits — the HLC logical-physical component, milliseconds;\nc: 16 bits — the HLC causality counter;\nnode: 10 bits — process/replica id (single-user local: derived from\npid+boot; multi-writer later: assigned);\nrandom: 36 bits — collision guard within one (l, c, node).\nThe (l, c) pair is a Hybrid Logical Clock (hlc-snap): on every local/send\nevent l:= max(l, pt), c increments only when l did not advance; on\nreceive l:= max(l, l_msg, pt) with the three-way c rule. Two theorems\ncarry the design (both machine-checked in../proofs/ ):\nCausality: e hb f ⇒ (l.e, c.e) <lex (l.f, c.f) — and because the\nencoding is big-endian fixed-width, plain string comparison of the body preserves that order (the pwc property: one < gives one-way causality).\nBoundedness: l ≥ pt always, |l − pt| bounded by the clock skew\nwindow, and c bounded — so 48+16 bits cannot overflow in practice and\nthe timestamp stays human-meaningful.\n3. The contract\nSortable = causal + temporal. ORDER BY id on any event table is a\ncausally consistent, roughly wall-clock order. Concurrent events order\narbitrarily but deterministically (node ‖ random tiebreak) — replay\nstability (A22).\nSnapshots for free. \"State as of t\" = fold events with hlc(id) ≤ (t, ∞); no coordination, no uncertainty wait (hlc-snap's\na-posteriori snapshot read). This is the join point with generation heads:\na generation's cut is an HLC value, and 03-storage.md §5 builds snapshot-reduced temporal queries on it.\nTyped at rest and in flight. IDs serialize as plain strings (YAML/JSON\n/SQL-friendly); the typed wrapper validates on parse and marshals\nlosslessly (zp0 typedterryid: TextMarshaler/Valuer pair, consistency\nround-trip tested). A simple untyped generator ships beside it for tooling\n(v1/v2 layering).\nNo semantic leakage. The ID carries kind + order + origin, never\ncontent — content addressing is a separate mechanism (hashes, 03-storage.md §3 ); conflating them would make renames\nre-identify records.\n3.5 Storage form — bare bodies, kind in the type\nDB columns persist the 22-char body only, never the kind prefix: the\nkind is carried by the column/table semantics (\"mem_facts.id is a fct\"), so\nstoring it per row would be redundant — and worse, it breaks ordering, since\nfull ids compare by prefix letter first while bodies compare by HLC. The TerryId helper keeps kind and body as separate fields — sorting\nand cuts key on body alone (valid across kinds: event cuts vs generation\nids vs fact intervals reduce to one string compare) — and recombines them\nonly in toString() for display and wire output. Surfaces accept either\nform on input ( coerce / bodyOf ) and always print the full form.\n4. Clock discipline\nThe domain layer never reads Date.now(); a Clock capability is injected\n(architecture layer rule), and the HLC state is process-singleton behind it.\nReplay mode substitutes the recorded clock events, making time itself an\nevent-log input (A22). Regression tests pin: monotonicity across a simulated\nNTP step backwards, c reset on pt catch-up, and cross-process merge via\nthe receive rule.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/03-storage.html":{"title":"Storage — SQLite SoT, derived indexes, hash addressing, temporal semantics","content":"1. The failure this closes\nAgent platforms scatter truth across a vector DB, a document store, a cache,\nand prompt-side summaries — then drift: the fresh fact is not yet embedded,\nthe deleted fact still ranks, the \"current\" summary describes last week, and\nnobody can say what the system believed when it made a decision. Temporal\ncorrectness has named bug classes even in mature databases — the aggregation gap (empty snapshots silently vanish from aggregates: \"no SP\nworker on duty between 00 and 03\" never appears) and the bag-difference\nbug — that every ad-hoc timeline implementation re-commits (bag-, Table 1:\nsix published systems, none free of both). Aletheia's storage plane fixes the\ntopology (one SoT, everything else derived) and adopts the temporal\ncorrectness contract wholesale.\n2. Topology Tier Technology Role Mutability\nSource of truth\nSQLite (WAL)\nevent logs, canonical rows (facts, runs, entries), generation catalog, heads\nappend + interval-close only\nDerived indexes\nFAISS (HNSW cosine), BM25 (lexical), SQLite projections\nretrieval acceleration\ngeneration-swapped, rebuildable\nBlobs\ncontent-addressed files under.theia/objects/\nartifacts, chunks, index shards\nimmutable\nAll three sit behind storage interfaces in packages/schema — the seam\nexists because the recurring change is \"another backend\" (D2): the planned\nPostgres deployment (multi-user; local stores become cooperative in-progress\nstate) swaps store-sqlite without touching engines (A15). The interface is\ncapability-shaped (event log, row store, vector index, lexical index, blob\nstore); the sqlite backend's behavioral tests double as the conformance\nsuite a second backend must pass — the artifact-that-runs discipline\napplied to the seam itself.\n3. Hash addressing — change detection you can trust\nEvery derived artifact records the digests of its inputs; every blob is named\nby its own digest ( objects/sha256/<2>/<62> ).\nAn intent-index entry 's key inputs are the file's content hash + the\nbuilder version + the prompt hash — if none changed, the entry is provably\ncurrent with zero model calls (AOCI's O(1) incremental maintenance made\nmechanical, A5).\nAn index generation records the set-hash of its member row digests —\nthe reconciler's diff is a hash compare, not a scan.\nThe event log is hash-chained (each event carries prev_digest ) —\ntamper-evidence for the audit chain ( 09-observability.md §4 ).\nEmbedding rows record (provider, model, dims) — a dimension or provider\nchange is a new generation, never an in-place re-embed (A21).\n4. Generations and heads (the A1 contract, storage view)\nThe generations catalog is one table: (gen_id terryid, concern, builder_version, input_digest, status, created_at); per-concern heads hold the served\npointer. Index shards, tree summaries, graph edges, and Σ snapshots all carry build_gen and nullable delete_gen; a reader at head H sees build_gen ≤ H AND (delete_gen IS NULL OR delete_gen > H) — the tombstone\nlifecycle that made the Fidant edge build atomic (verified there: head\nmonotonic, zero double-live), generalized to every derived surface. Publish is UPDATE heads SET gen=? WHERE concern=? AND gen=? — one OCC compare-and-set;\nGC collects orphans level-triggered. FAISS shards are immutable files named by\ngeneration: the vector index for gen N is objects/…/faiss-<concern>-<gen>;\n\"updating\" the index means building a new shard set and flipping the head —\nMV-PBT's append-only partition discipline at file granularity, which also\nmakes index corruption impossible by construction (a crash leaves an\nunreferenced file).\n5. Temporal semantics — snapshot reducibility as the spec\nInterval-stamped rows ( [valid_from, valid_to) in HLC space) + the\ngeneration head give Aletheia a bitemporal store: event time (what was true\nof the world) and system time (what Aletheia believed — the HLC of the\nrecording event), kept separate exactly as MemForest separates source-time\nfrom event-time and bag- separates the axes.\nThe contract (bag-, adopted as law):\nSnapshot reducibility. For any supported temporal query Q and time t: snap_t(Q(temporal relations)) = Q(snap_t(relations)). This single\nequation is the spec; the golden test suite instantiates it per operator.\nNo aggregation gaps. Aggregates over periods with empty input yield\nrows (count = 0 with their interval) — the AG bug class is tested for by\nconstruction, with bag-'s worked example as the fixture.\nUnique encoding. Query results coalesce adjacent intervals with equal\nannotations (K-coalescing), so two equivalent stores render byte-identical\nanswers — a determinism requirement, not aesthetics (A22).\nImplementation shape. Rows carry [valid_from, valid_to) interval\ncolumns; as-of reads admit by the cut (index generations carry interval\nsidecars so vector/lexical lanes filter before load, and the load step\nre-checks the DB — the truth for post-build supersessions). bag-'s SQL\nrewriting templates (change-point extraction + window functions) are the\nadopted implementation path for richer temporal operators (interval\naggregation, coalesced projections) as they land.\nWhat this buys, concretely: \"which constraints did the run see when it\napproved the plan?\" is memory AS OF run.plan_approved_hlc; \"when did we\nbelieve X?\" is an interval query; a post-incident replay reads the store the\nrun actually read. MemForest's admitted gap — no MVCC, ingest blocks queries\nbehind a process lock — is closed by reading through (head, HLC) pairs:\nwriters append, readers see the last published cut, and nobody blocks anybody.\n6. Write-path law\nRepos are the only SQL homes; engines own transactions via withTx (closure-scoped — a tx cannot leak).\nEvery retriable write derives its idempotency key from business identity\n( digest(kind, subject, gen) ), checked in the same tx — replays converge\n(cloud-billing Operation pattern).\nRows with ≥3 lifecycle states get an FSM transition table + guard walked\nby a test (P6); status writes route through the guard.\nBlobs upload before the tx that references them — a failure leaves GC-able\ngarbage, never a row without bytes.\nDeletes are interval-closes or tombstones; physical deletion is GC's job,\nafter the audit retention window ( 11-governance.md ).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/04-model-gateway.html":{"title":"Model gateway — the one seam all model traffic crosses","content":"1. The failure this closes\nWhen every engine calls providers directly, the cross-cutting obligations —\ncost metering, data-class masking, retry policy, uncertainty capture,\nrecord/replay, audit hashing — become per-caller disciplines, and per-caller\ndisciplines rot (P2). The reference systems each solved a slice: overseer's Harness interface normalizes four CLI agents behind one seam; claudexor's\nconstitution separates routing (who executes) from strategy (how many\nrun) and forbids adapters from orchestrating; psp put 69 providers behind one\ntagged result and kept the engine provider-agnostic. The gateway is that\nseam built once: a separate deployable service ( theia gateway, also\nembeddable in-process) that owns every completion, embedding, and harness\ninvocation in the system.\n2. The seam vocabulary\nOne tagged result across ALL providers and harnesses ( packages/schema ):\ntype CallResult <T> = | { kind: 'ok'; value: T; usage: Usage; uncertainty?: Uncertainty } | { kind: 'retryable'; reason: string; retryAfterMs?: number } | { kind: 'fatal'; reason: string } // bad request, auth, schema | { kind: 'needs_user'; reason: string } // interactive gate required | { kind: 'truncated'; partial: string; usage: Usage } | { kind: 'refused'; reason: string } // provider safety refusal\nAdapters classify; callers own time (P8): zero retry loops inside an\nadapter; the kernel's retry/backoff/fallback chains consume retryable. refused is its own kind because it is neither retryable nor a caller bug\nand must be scored honestly (the ledger paper scored Fable refusals as\nfailures rather than silently substituting a model — we keep that law).\nUsage is normalized (input, cacheRead, cacheWrite, output) tokens plus\na locked USD cost synthesized from an embedded price table at call time\n(overseer's pattern: never read cost from the stream — subscriptions carry\nnone — and never recompute historical cost after a price change; the same\nno-reprojection law as the wallet-ledger rule).\nUncertainty is token-logprob-derived ( u = −log Avg(softmax(logprobs)),\nuac) where the transport exposes logprobs; absent otherwise — never faked.\nConsumers calibrate thresholds per stage from run history (A11).\nStructured output is validated at this seam: JSON replies parse through\none schema-validating helper (strict schemas, length caps, enum sync);\nfailures classify as fatal(schema) with a sanitized summary — model text\nnever rides an exception message into logs.\n3. Transports Transport Mechanism Sessions Notes\nharness-codex\nCodex CLI exec --json / OAuth ChatGPT subscription over the Responses API (axio-transport-codex shape)\n✓ resume\nselectable first-class family (with claude: `theia init --harness codex\nharness-claude\nClaude Code CLI claude -p --output-format stream-json --verbose as a subprocess ( CLI-first, not SDK — subscription-quota path with exact codex-exec parity: jail-able, stream-parsed, overseer's claude driver the reference); a PTY wrapper is the NAMED future fallback if print-mode gaps appear; the SDK is not used\n✓ resume ( --resume <id> preserves the session — probed 2.1.252)\nsubscription auth NEVER falls back to an API key (THE-013 — ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is stripped from the child env, and the doctor demands a logged-in claude.ai session); session lineage chained by content hash (meridian lineage.ts shape — pure, testable); tool whitelist via probed --allowedTools / --disallowedTools (the probed tool-governance ladder, 10-integrations.md §3 ); three registered postures: claude (judging: Bash/Read/Grep/Glob), claude-write (probed headless acceptEdits ), and claude-index (budget-light indexing: sonnet default, NO tools, MAX_THINKING_TOKENS=0 — the 34938 quota receipt: 2,049 files at the opus judging posture locked $123 and ate a Max subscription; init seeds indexer: claude-index for the claude family)\nharness-fake\nrecorded golden wire fixtures / synthesized weighted verdicts\n✓\nthe simulator & replay seam (A22); byte-asserted fixtures per operation (psp)\nembeddings-local\nONNX model on this machine (onnxruntime + a pure-JS tokenizer; mean/CLS pooling, unit vectors)\nn/a\nthe default embedder ( gateway.embeddings.provider: local ): zero USD, offline once fetched; the closed registry ships all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d, default) and bge-small-en-v1.5 (384d); a model fetches itself into ~/.cache/theia/models on first use with one notice line, or via theia setup --fetch-embeddings [model]; autoDownload: false makes a missing model a loud error\napi-openai\ndirect API (embeddings; optional completions)\nn/a\ntext-embedding-3-small default; selected by gateway.embeddings.provider: openai; needs the user's key — without it the vector lane is off with the reason printed, never a silent switch of provider\nThe harness interface is overseer's, ported: args/sessionArgs, parseStreamLine (normalize native streams into typed events), parseSessionId, classifyFault (transient vs fatal), jailRwPaths, defaultModel(role). Adapters are translational only — no budgets, no\nwinner selection, no review policy (claudexor INV-013); a harness is not a\nrole, and no role is hardcoded to a harness (INV-010): the gateway routes by (intent, policy, availability), and availability is doctor-verified (a probe call), never assumed from config (INV-011/012 — an unavailable\nharness is visible with a reason, never silently substituted).\n4. Embeddings\nOne EmbeddingTransport interface: (texts, purpose) → vectors, with the (provider, model, dims) triple recorded on every stored vector and on every\nindex generation ( 03-storage.md §3 ). Changing dimensions\nor provider is therefore a new generation build behind the head — a seamless\nswap by construction, not a migration script. Batching, rate limits, and\nbacklog degradation (missing embedding ⇒ row drops out of vector search only;\nlexical still serves) are gateway concerns, invisible to engines.\nThe provider is workspace policy ( gateway.embeddings, 15 §3 )\nbecause it is generation identity, and the CLI resolves it in ONE place\n( apps/theia/src/embeddings.ts ) for every verb that builds or queries a\nvector generation. Two laws hold there: (1) availability is honest — the\nopenai posture without a key resolves to no embedder with the reason, the\nlocal posture fetches its model or refuses loudly, and read verbs degrade to\nthe lexical lanes saying so; (2) identity at the read seam — a vector\nindex is queried only by the triple that built it (the generation's recorded\nbuilder version); a mismatch drops the vector lane with a notice naming the\nreindex verb, because a foreign embedder returns confidently wrong\nneighbours, which is worse than lexical-only.\n5. What lives at the seam by construction\nData-class masking ( 11-governance.md §4 ):\nevery outbound prompt passes the class gate — Prohibited content blocks\nthe call ( fatal(policy) ), Restricted content is masked before the bytes\nleave the process. One choke point; callers cannot forget.\nAudit hashing: every call emits a gateway event (model id, prompt\ndigest, params, usage, result kind, latencies) into the hash-chained log —\nthe competitor's agent_run_id + prompt_version + input hashes made\nmechanical.\nRecord/replay: in record mode, full request/response pairs land in the\nblob store keyed by digest; in replay mode the gateway serves them back\nand refuses live calls — replay equality is testable\n( 08-evaluation.md §2 ).\nMetering: per-run budgets enforced here (cost cap = policy value);\nexceeding one yields fatal(budget) — loud, attributable, resumable.\nTracing: one OTel span per call, usage and cost as attributes, prompt\npayloads by blob reference only ( 09-observability.md ).\nPricing without code: a kind: Pricing manifest overlays the\nembedded price table (per-model USD/MTok rates) and sets per-transport costBasis — list keeps adapter-locked figures winning; actual declares the manifest rates the true marginal cost, overriding even an\nadapter figure (a subscription seat may declare its models at 0). Every gateway.call records priceSource ( adapter | manifest | embedded ),\nso receipts stay attributable across rate changes; the cost-lock law is\nuntouched — locked once at call time, never recomputed (THE-015).\n6. Routing and strategy are orthogonal (claudexor INV-140)\nRouting picks WHO executes (harness + credential profile + model) — set by\npolicy or explicit choice: gateway.routing[role] names the transport and gateway.models[role] the model (resolved in the router, part of the replay\nkey — an explicit request model wins). Strategy picks HOW MANY units run and how results\ncombine (single / best-of / council / delegation depth) — set by the kernel\nper task class on the cost-accuracy frontier (A9: the manager scaffold buys\nmost where the unaided model is weakest; strong-model small tasks route\nsingle). No control mixes the axes.\n7. Session continuity — the Σ-shared stable prefix\nRe-cut per the chief's 2026-09-03 decision\n( notes/15-session-continuity-sigma.md ):\nthe accumulating resume mode (and its optimistic state-hash registry)\nis REMOVED — it carried round-to-round state PAST the Σ projection, the\nplatform's quality instrument (ledger §4 corpse marker). What follows is\nthe shipped replacement.\nCold dispatch conflates two costs, and continuity may only touch the\nfirst: re-send cost (the stable context re-transmitted every round —\neliminated by provider prompt caching WHEN the prompt is ordered\nstable-prefix-first) and re-exploration cost (the model re-running\nlast round's greps — whose correct home is a curated store that\nre-projects, never an accumulating transcript). The mechanism:\nEvery dispatch prompt is two spans ( assembleJobPromptParts ): the\nSTABLE span first — task frame, tier note, repo map/context, the\ndurable Σ core, byte-identical across rounds — then the VOLATILE delta\n— attempt, Σ projection, folded feedback, rescope budget. This\nordering is unconditional (a pure win): the previous assembly put the\nattempt counter in byte 15 and the map last, so no two rounds ever\nshared a cacheable prefix. Delta-last also lands the course\ncorrections where recency-weighted attention is strongest.\nThe base advances only on a legitimate Σ event — a rescope\nrewrites the frame, a re-index or diff-triggered context entry changes\nthe map. Each advance changes the audited prefix digest; between\nadvances every round of a task shares one prefix.\nrun.sessionContinuity: off | sigma-shared (default off until the\nreceipt; --sessions / --no-sessions override). sigma-shared arms\nthe audit: every dispatch emits task.note {source: session_prefix, taskId, role, digest, stableChars, deltaChars} — byte-stability of\nthe shared prefix across rounds is then a query over the run's own\nnotes, and \"Σ is provably the sole quality input\" is checkable from\nthe exchange blobs. Dispatch BYTES are identical under both modes.\nThe round-to-round carry is fold-governed, deterministic (notes/15 §9): a re-raised review objection folds into its standing\nfeedback entry under the platform's one paraphrase bar (token-set\nJaccard ≥ 0.92 — the memory write gate's), newest wording wins, and\nthe count renders as \"raised N rounds running\" — the review-loop\ngrounding a cold round structurally loses, carried THROUGH Σ, bounded\nat 3 distinct objections. A Self-GC-shaped planner over delta objects\nis the designed, receipt-gated escalation — never a third mode.\nBase-session forking (manufacture a harness session ending exactly\nat the stable prefix; fork it per round) is the designed second path\nfor prefix reuse beyond the provider cache TTL — per-harness probes\nbefore any fork verb exists (T-O law); until then the honest posture\nis cold + prompt ordering, never silent accumulation.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/06-orchestration.html":{"title":"Orchestration — kernel, roles, planning state, delegation","content":"1. The failure this closes\nFlat agent loops fail three ways at once: the transcript outgrows the window\nand poisons decisions (0.90 → 0.74 accuracy over horizon, 0.53 under noise —\nSKILL.state), early goals drift out of scope and the agent loops on a blocked\nstation forever (ReCAP's ReAct traces; a 250k-token run repeating one\nhypothesis line 7,743 times — ledger §4.2), and free-form multi-agent systems\nadd their own anomaly classes — infinite delegation chains, premature stops,\nzombie children, emergent message storms (ReDel's taxonomy, confirmed by the\nAgentOps survey). The kernel answers with owned coordination over bounded\nstate: one coordinator, an event-sourced phase FSM, stateless role pools,\ntyped verdicts, and a planning state that never grows with the transcript.\n2. The kernel shape (A8)\nThe contract, numbered:\nA single coordinator per run owns all ticket/task state — there are no\nlocks because there is no sharing (overseer's scheduler law). Pools are\nstateless: they run a harness job to a recognized verdict and reply on one\nchannel; agents never touch run state directly.\nPersisted state is the event log; the phase of every task is a phase event. Restart = replay. The in-memory dispatch map\n( dispatchable | scheduled ) is coordinator-only and rebuilt on boot.\nPhases are an FSM as data (P6): transition table + guard in packages/kernel, walked by a test (every state reachable, no dangling\ntransitions). Ambiguity is a state ( uncertain outcomes get a\nreconciliation step, not a blind retry); invariant breach freezes the task\ninto an inspectable escalated state with a repair path — never\nbest-effort continuation.\nVerdicts are a closed vocabulary per role ( packages/schema ):\ne.g. implementer → ready | cant_do | algedonic; reviewer → approve | rework | discard; verifier → pass | fail(evidence); arbiter\n→ continue | escalate. The algedonic verdict (overseer, from VSM) is\nthe systemic-trouble bypass: it skips review/merge and wakes the lead role\nfor a root-cause re-scope rather than another iteration.\nGuards bound every loop: round budgets per task, a no-progress guard\n(re-issuing the identical task halts the loop), a rescope budget\n( run.maxRescopes — a lead rescope resets the round budget only that\nmany times, then the guard forces discard), the dispatch-stall guard ( run.stallPauseAfter consecutive no_progress outcomes — gateway\nfailures, off-contract replies — pause the run resumable with one dispatch_stalled anomaly, because the escalated↔no_progress cycle has\nno round-budget exit), wall/cost caps as policy values, and a cut-off\nsummarizer so a token-capped worker still reports (each guard exists\nbecause its absence was measured — ledger §1/§4).\nA planned verdict with no subtasks cannot silently kill a task. The planner's planned means \"I am decomposing\" and carries the\nsubtasks; a bare planned would end the task with a plan and no code —\nthe no-op the vendored planner law warns against. The coordinator\ncoerces a subtask-less planned to ready (the plan is already in Σ;\nthe implementer acts on it) and raises a planned_without_subtasks anomaly, so a mis-following planner is visible, never silent (P10 —\nmeasured live on an approved --from-plan run whose full_spec tasks\nre-planned and self-terminated).\nOff-contract replies are a repair case, never a verdict. A reply that\nparses to no legal verdict gets ONE bounded repair naming the exact zod\npaths (or the legal verdict words) on the same harness session; a second\nmiss freezes the task naming both problems — a finished piece of work\nmust not read as no_progress because its JSON wore prose (the $1.83/miss\nlive receipt, ledger §4).\n3. Execution state Σ (A6)\nΣ is the run's typed working state — the DuMate tuple hardened by the\nSKILL.state runtime contract:\nΣ = ⟨ task context (goal, track, DoD — immutable), plan DAG (nodes: subtasks with depth + status; edges: deps), evidence base (artifact refs, verdicts, hashes — append-only), guidance (persistent rubric + ephemeral rubric) ⟩\nPrompts carry (spec, Σ-projection, latest observation) — never\ntranscripts. The projection is ReCAP's path discipline: the root→current\nplan path, the sibling summary, and the task-scoped evidence — O(depth),\nnot O(history). Intermediate reasoning is discarded after its ∆Σ patch is\nvalidated (bounded prompts: 16× token reduction at horizon 100, accuracy\nup, in SKILL.state's measurements).\nAll mutation is validated patches. A role returns ∆Σ; the kernel owns\nthe schema, validates, applies Σ ⊕ ∆Σ, and rolls back invalid patches\nwith a retry — a malformed model output cannot corrupt state. The merge\noperator ⊕ (key-wise LWW by HLC, null = delete) is proved deterministic,\nidempotent, and convergent for concurrent key-disjoint patches in../proofs/state_merge.v (A7) — the exact gap\nSKILL.state names for multi-agent use.\nΣ is derived state. The event log remains the SoT; Σ is a fold over it\n(P7), which closes SKILL.state's own limitation list: late-relevance\nobservations and \"history is the deliverable\" tasks (audits, postmortems)\nread the log, not Σ.\nPlan mutation is one operator. Replanning is Π(plan, evidence,\nguidance) — it may expand, prune, or rewire pending nodes; executed\nnodes are monotone and evidence is never recomputed (DuMate's law). Only\nthe ready frontier (pending nodes with satisfied deps) is\ndispatchable; plan-ahead + head-execution + refine-on-return is the ReCAP\ncycle expressed on the DAG.\nRubrics steer and stop. The persistent rubric (from goal + track) is\nthe quality scaffold every role sees; the ephemeral rubric is regenerated\nfrom the evidence base each cycle and doubles as the stopping predicate —\n\"no outstanding gap\" ends the loop (DuMate), bounded by the round budget.\nClarify — the needs_user arm. A planning-authority role that\ncannot proceed without the human replies clarify with typed questions —\nnever a guess. The coordinator consults the injected interaction seam\nBEFORE the FSM: answered Q&A lands as a user.note event, folds into Σ\n( clarifications.<taskId>, accumulated), and becomes a memory fact so it\nis never asked twice; unanswered (non-interactive, no TTY, declined), the\ntask freezes to escalated with the questions on the verdict event and a clarify_unanswered anomaly — visible and resumable. The lead's next\ndispatch is TOLD nobody answered; a second consecutive unanswered\nclarify on the same task pauses the run resumable ( clarify_stalled ) —\nthe clarify↔escalate spin has no round-budget exit, and an operator\ndecision is the only honest move left (the ~13-round live receipt,\nledger §1).\n4. Roles\nRoles are intents, never harnesses (claudexor INV-010); the gateway routes\neach to a harness by policy. The standing set (per-role prompts user-editable\nin.theia/prompts/ — 10-integrations.md §4 ):\nRole Produces Discipline\nlead\ntask ops (new/update/replace/cancel), goal verdict, accept (standing work certified verified-complete → the merge path, dependents unblock; refused without a verify_pass — the 34521 receipt: verified-done work discarded for want of the verb strands every dependent), verify rewrite on a rescope ( \"verify\": […] replaces the task's commands — the broken-check lever, recorded on the audited verdict)\nsingle planning authority; consumes escalations + algedonic wakes in batched context; rules OUT a broken check (aborted runs, missing frameworks in the verifier evidence) before rescoping the work\nplanner\nplan.md / plan-DAG nodes\nresearches via the codebase model, not raw exploration\nimplementer\ndiffs + tests in an isolated worktree\nfresh session per task (ralphex's context-degradation law); squash-rebase onto trunk\nfresh-perspective implementer\nindependent attempt — no inherited worker notes, but guidance.* directives (user steering, lead rescope direction) AND the task's rejecting-verdict feedback (last 3 notes, foldFeedback — live ≡ rehydrated by one shared fold) survive the strip\ndiversification against anchored-wrong-plan regressions (the ledger paper's own fix, adopted as a role); fresh of its own history, never deaf to the lead\nreviewer\napprove/rework/discard + cited evidence\nlevel-k adversary (gr2): hunts the producer's known failure modes (the anomaly taxonomy as checklist), never judges plausibility (A12)\nverifier\npass/fail from EXECUTED tests\ngenerates tests when none exist; its result is ground truth and overrides any \"done\" claim (ledger step-5 law; claudexor INV-043: tool success is evidence, not prose)\nmerger\nserial trunk integration\npool size 1; baseline-tests → merge → re-test; only the coordinator writes trunk\narbiter\ncontinue/escalate on any disagreement\nbreaks review/merge ties\ndiagnostician\nroot-cause report\non repeated failure: KRCA narrowing over the trace tree + skeleton causal prior + per-modality evidence agents; results memorized with graph-fingerprint reuse (A13, A14)\n5. Delegation and strategy\nDelegation is a policy-bounded tool, not model discretion (A8): a role\nmay request sub-runs ( delegate = spawn, wait = deferred collect —\nReDel's two schemes), but depth, fan-out, and budget are policy values, and\nthe coordinator owns the children's lifecycle (no zombies — every child is a\ntracked task; the subreaper analogue at process level lives in the runner).\nStrategy (single / best-of / council / delegation depth) is chosen per task\nclass on the measured cost-accuracy frontier (A9): the scaffold pays most\nwhere the unaided model is weakest (+23.4pp on a 27B model, +8 on a frontier\none, negative on strong-model small tasks — ledger), so the default is\nsingle-shot for trivial tasks, manager-scaffold for oversized ones, and\ncouncil only where a golden benchmark shows it pays. Workflow synthesis (composing new role graphs from a task spec, validating on example pairs,\nIPR-refining prompts) is the layer above, gated experimental (A10).\n6. Uncertainty gating (A11)\nEach verdict carries the gateway's uncertainty when available; per-stage\nthresholds are calibrated from run history (the quantile-of-correct method,\nuac) and stored as policy values. Above-threshold outputs trigger targeted\nverification (an extra verifier probe, a retrieval-augmented recheck) —\nnever blanket double-checks (D7). Sub-threshold flows proceed; the\ncalibration loop is an eval-subsystem job, so thresholds carry receipts like\nany other knob.\n7. Plan mode — the approval gate as an artifact\ntheia plan (or run --plan ) runs context collection and the planner role\nONLY: the blueprint projection, memory recall, and AST facts render with\nprovenance; the decomposition lands as the plan DAG with per-task\ntrack/verify and an honest cost line; the whole spec persists as a content-hashed plan artifact. Plan context is UNCAPPED (run/plan\nparity, 2026-09-07): the planner sees the same complete blueprint and\nwhole context entries a run dispatch injects — the size guards are the\nsame loud thresholds ( contextAlertChars / contextRefuseChars ) plus the run.blueprintMinImportance floor, never a silent schema cut — so the\ndigest attests what the planner actually read. A task's verify is one command or an\nORDERED LIST (focused suite first, full suite as the regression guard) —\nbecause a check is never a task: a task whose only work is running tests\nproduces no diff for the implementer and nothing for the reviewer, and the\nprompt contract forbids authoring one (the run 2ak7ylmqu6 receipt,\nledger §4). theia run --from-plan <id> refuses any\nstatus but approved and stamps every created task with the plan digest it\nsatisfies; any edit (including add-context ) produces a NEW digest back in draft — approval cannot silently survive a change (the INV-081 shape;\nthis is what makes the full-spec track's spec-approved gate a mechanism, 11-governance.md §3 ). Approve / request-changes /\nadd-context are verbs on the artifact.\ntheia run --from-plan also holds the budget against the plan's history-derived cost floor (tasks × the store's recorded avg $/task —\nnever fabricated; a cold store has no floor): a default budget below the\nfloor refuses before the first call, an explicit --budget proceeds with\na loud warning — an under-budgeted run dies mid-implementation with the\nround's work stranded (the spqr-010 fullspec-v2 receipt, notes/31).\nAuthored verify commands are pre-flighted at base when the plan is\ncreated under run.planVerifyPreflight: always (opt-in for now, default disable — the pre-flight executes plan-authored commands before any\napproval exists, so it runs only on the operator's standing consent, the indexPolicy bar; disabled with verifies present prints one hint line, and\nthe demo posture opts in): each distinct command runs once at its root, and the observations\n(exit code, labeled stdout/stderr tails) land on the plan's event scope —\noutside the hashed spec, so an observation never moves the approval digest —\nand render under plan show. Pre-flight is an observation, never a gate: a\nred test at base may be the task's very point (red→green), but \"aborted:\nmissing framework\" at base means the CHECK is broken, and the approver sees\nit before a run spends rounds on it. Mid-run, the same failure class is the\nlead's to fix: a rescope may carry a verify rewrite (§4), recorded on the\nverdict and folded from the log by live dispatch and resume alike.\n8. Run lifecycle — pause, resume, steer, ask\nRestart is replay. All coordinator state derives from the event log\n( fromEvents ); theia run --resume <runId> rehydrates and continues —\nstop-midway-then-resume is provably equal to never-stopped. A first\nSIGINT is a graceful stop (in-flight verdicts land, run.paused ); a\nsecond is a hard stop (harness process GROUPS killed — children a plain\nkill would miss). A hard kill at ANY point still leaves a resumable run by construction: every event commits in its own WAL transaction, so\nthe log is always a chain-intact prefix, and resume depends only on the\nlog, never on the pause marker. The cost of a hard kill is in-flight\nattempts only — the attempt is the atom: a killed attempt's partial\nworkspace edits are reset on the next dispatch, a stale merge state is\naborted on merger entry, and the remaining budget re-derives from the\nrecorded gateway events.\nSteering is an audited event. theia msg <runId> [\"--task <id>\"] \"…\" appends a user.note that folds into Σ guidance — run-level, or\nprojected only into one task's jobs; the routing decision is recorded ON\nthe note, and a note to a terminal task reroutes to run-level guidance\nnaming the original addressee. Delivery is at dispatch boundaries (the\nattempt is the atom — there is no mid-turn injection surface). In the\nlive TUI the fold is immediate; cross-process on a live run it lands at\nthe next rehydration, and the CLI says so.\nAsides never touch the run. theia probe <runId> \"q\" is a\nfork-lite: rehydrated Σ + one read-only cited call in the probe's own\nscope with a lineage event — the parent's log is byte-identical by\nconstruction. theia ask \"…\" is the run-less sibling: one cited answer\nover the retrieval union (memory + intent index + run history), and an\nempty union abstains at $0 — the model is never called, so an\nunsupported question cannot hallucinate.\n9. Forking — counterfactuals on live history\ntheia fork <runId> [--at <eventId>] --with \"…\" creates a new run whose\nlog is the parent's prefix up to a verdict boundary (fork points are\nwhere workspace state re-materializes from recorded commit shas;\nmid-attempt moments are deliberately not forkable) plus a lineage event and\nthe injected divergence. The hybrid gateway mode makes forks near-free:\nreplay on recording hit, live-and-record on miss — the shared prefix\nre-serves the parent's recorded replies, only the divergent suffix bills.\nStrict replay stays the untouched determinism gate (THE-043). theia compare <runA> <runB> reports the divergence point, cost delta\npost-baseline, outcome delta, and artifact diff — the paired-ablation\nprotocol applied WITHIN a run: controlled A/B of a live decision with the\nprefix held byte-identical. Fork lineage renders as a tree in show;\nsibling outcomes feed experiential memory (the AMER resume-at-divergence\narm made literal).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/07-memory.html":{"title":"Memory — canonical facts, scoped trees, temporal retrieval","content":"This doc is the shipped, code-verified memory subsystem. Its\ndesigned evolution — the world-view layer, adaptive retrieval, context\ngovernance, documents, and rules/skills — is the reviewed design set: 18-memory-structure.md, 19-documents.md, 20-rules-skills.md (analysis: notes/09 ).\n1. The failure this closes\nAgent memory systems compound four losses: extraction-only memory discards\nthe verbatim wording temporal and quote questions need (summarization-based\nmemory: 14% exact-match vs 91% verbatim on constraint recall — a 77-point\nfidelity gap), serialized LLM-in-the-loop write paths delay when evidence\nbecomes queryable (MemForest measured 6–9.5× build-rate gaps against systems\nwith extraction on the write path), latest-state summaries overwrite the\nintermediate states that \"where did Bob live before Miami\" needs, and flat\nvector pools retrieve the wrong time because similarity is blind to position.\nAletheia's memory subsystem is the synthesis of MemForest's write path, the\nFidant engine's receipt-carrying retrieval doctrine, and the storage plane's\ntemporal semantics — with the deltas each source lacks alone.\n2. The write path (MemForest, adopted)\nParallel extraction. Sessions split into small chunks extracted\nconcurrently — no serialized autoregressive pass; the critical path scales\nwith incoming content, not accumulated state.\nCanonical facts are the stable write unit: retrieval-ready text,\nprovenance refs, entity mentions, topical signals, confidence — and TWO\ntimestamps, source_hlc (when recorded) and event_time (what the fact\nasserts), never conflated ( 03-storage.md §5 ).\nCanonicalization merges only semantic equivalents (normalized exact match\n→ embedding candidates → bounded LLM check); a changed value is a new\ntemporally-anchored fact, never an overwrite — old states remain leaves.\nTwo substrates, always. Typed canonical facts AND verbatim chunks\n(with PII span redaction at write — the chunk keeps the dialogue, loses\nonly the secret). The Fidant receipt is decisive: chunks alone carried\n0.659 composite where extraction was unavailable; extraction alone scored\n0.275 vs the full stack's 0.789.\nScoped trees. Facts route (no extra LLM calls) into two tree\nfamilies — session trees (source order) and entity trees (subject state\nevolution); leaves are time-ordered, group nodes summarize intervals,\nroots serve coarse recall and positional browse. Insertion marks dirty\nancestor paths; summaries refresh lazily on mem maintain and the\npost-run sweep — a new leaf recomputes exactly its touched path (the\ncall-counted receipt: 1 leaf ⇒ 2 summaries). The multi-entity\norganizing layer formerly sketched as a scene/topic family is now the\nworld-view group layer of 18-memory-structure.md §2, queued behind\na corpus that needs it.\nTrees are derived state. Persistent state (facts, assignments,\nstructure, refs) is SoT; summaries, embeddings, and index rows are\ngeneration-published behind heads (A1) — closing MemForest's own gap: our\nreaders get MVCC-style consistent cuts and never block behind ingest.\n3. Retrieval architecture — lanes over generations\nRetrieval is a union of lanes over generation-published indexes: the\nlexical lane (tantivy BM25 over terms from ONE analyzer — @theia/text-analysis, per-token script + per-document language Snowball\nstemming shared by index build, query and the scan lanes; memory.analyzer selects it, its version rides the generation's builder version and a reader\nwith another analyzer reads plain with a notice — the bench receipt:\nlexical R@10 74.4 → 77.7, notes/10 §4), the vector lane (FAISS HNSW cosine,\npresent when the generation carries embeddings — the local ONNX embedder by\ndefault, 04 §4 ), the opt-in sentence lane\n( memory.sentenceGraph: sentences under the chunks, kNN edges, parent\nchunks admitted by aggregated sentence similarity — 18 §2.3;\nmeasured net-negative on conversational chunks, off pending the\ndocument-element revival — ledger §4), the event-time lane\n( memory.timeLane, default on: explicit absolute dates in the ask,\nregex-only, union rows whose ASSERTED [event_from, event_to) falls in\nthe range — §6, receipt 1 W / 0 L / 214 T), and the recency lane (a rank\nsignal, never a filter), fused by rank (RRF, K=60) and ordered\nconsensus-first — a candidate found by more lanes outranks any single-lane\nscore. Read-side near-dup collapse unions lane signals into the newest row\n(duplication is evidence, not noise). Indexes cover ALL rows — superseded\nincluded — with a [valid_from, valid_to) interval sidecar per ref, which\nis what makes as-of reads first-class lanes ( bm25@gen / vector@gen:\nover-fetch, sidecar-filter by the cut, DB re-check at load); rows written\nafter a generation reach reads through the scan lanes until the next\nreindex — a stated boundary, not a surprise. A degraded store degrades\nhonestly: no vector generation ⇒ lexical-only; no generation at all ⇒\nentry scan.\nThe fact-edge graph — hop pre-computation at ingest (per-fact\npseudo-queries ANN-linked, density-capped, tombstoned generations; the\nsource receipt: multi-hop 0.617 → 0.842 on the origin engine) — is adopted\ndesign queued behind a multi-hop workload that earns it\n( 13-roadmap.md ); nothing in the shipped laws depends\non it.\n4. Three memory tiers for the platform itself (KRCA/AMER)\nBeyond user/project memory, the platform writes its own scopes: working state lives in Σ and dies with the run; the diagnostics scope memorizes every root-cause analysis under a canonical failure\nfingerprint, so a repeated failure shape is recognized ( reused ) instead\nof re-derived — the AMER skip path, shipped for both the CLI verb and the\nMCP run_diagnose (host models and the kernel recognize each other's\nfingerprints); session hooks distill harness sessions into checkpoint\nchunks and recalled context. The full factual/experiential split with\nresume-at-divergence reuse (A14) deepens as diagnosis volume accrues; the\nfingerprint store is its foundation.\n5. Retrieval doctrine — the laws with receipts\nPorted from the origin engine's ledger as laws. Laws 1–6 govern the\nshipped lanes today; laws 7–9 bind the lanes that adopt them (force-loads,\nsufficiency, block concentration arrive with the retrieval stages that\nneed them — each must re-earn its receipt on our benchmarks first):\nUnion, never replacement — lanes union and rerank; a candidate\nfound by more lanes outranks a one-lane hit (consensus-first; the\nadditive blend measured worse at the source and stays off).\nWiden the first stage, not the LLM pool — recall breadth before the\nreranker (their depth finding); rerank fires only above the prune budget.\nRRF fuses incomparable scales (rank-based, K=60); lexical and vector\nscores never mix arithmetically.\nDuplication is a ranking signal — near-dup collapse unions lane\nsignals into the newest row instead of dropping (loss-free at the store).\nRecency ranks, never filters — an old-but-relevant fact must surface.\nStructure is routing, never evidence — tree descent lands on leaves;\nsummaries guide, only members answer (SGMem's h=1 law: one hop of untyped\nsimilarity is signal, two is drift).\nForce-loads for cue-disconnected evidence — constraints on\ndecision-support asks, identifier-bearing rows, document-majority loads;\nforced rows are exempt from ranking budgets.\nSufficiency-gate the block — one cheap probe; if thin, ONE bounded\nretry with round-1 rows pinned; abstention (\"records do not cover this\")\nbeats assembling near-misses.\nConcentrate the block — admission-ordered budgets per origin class;\ndilution is the measured hallucination axis, and concentration reversed it\nwhere three scaffolds failed.\n6. Temporal queries\nEvery retrieval accepts an asOf cut (HLC + head): the lanes read the\nsnapshot the cut defines, so \"what did we believe when the plan was\napproved\" and \"what changed since the incident\" are first-class queries with\nsnapshot-reducibility guarantees ( 03-storage.md §5 ).\nTree browse answers positional temporal asks (the interval before a\ntransition) that flat similarity structurally cannot (MemForest's residence\nexample is the canonical fixture).\n7. Lifecycle\nLifecycle ops edit persistent state first and regenerate only\nintersecting derived artifacts — never a stream replay (MemForest).\nShipped: supersession with version chains (superseded versions are\nexcluded from every recall path while history stays queryable at its cut\n— agentmemory's law), mem maintain (tree-summary refresh + orphan\ngeneration GC, including index artifacts), and the post-run maintenance\nsweep. Targeted deletion by provenance ref (GDPR-shaped), merge, and decay\nfollow the same persistent-state-first law when they land\n( 13-roadmap.md ). Maintenance runs on the serve reconciler loop and the post-run sweep;\nevery gate and threshold in this subsystem is a policy value with a\nledger row ( 08-evaluation.md §4 ).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/11-governance.html":{"title":"Governance — tracks, gates, policy values, data classes","content":"1. The failure this closes\nGovernance-as-prose fails in both directions: rules nobody can check rot\n(the interpreter law, P2), and rules applied uniformly bureaucratize the\nsmall fix until engineers route around the whole system (the competitor's own\nanti-pattern list: \"Full Spec на каждый хотфикс\"). The plus8soft guidebooks\ndefine the right shape — differentiated tracks, named gates, human merge\nauthority, data classes, auditable runs — as checklists humans must remember.\nAletheia keeps the shape and gives every rule an interpreter: a policy value\nthe kernel consumes, a gate the FSM enforces, a mask the gateway applies.\n2. Tracks\nTwo delivery tracks, chosen at task creation (by the lead role or the human;\ndisputes → the stricter track). The planner assigns a track per task in\nplan mode, the value is part of the content-hashed plan artifact, and its\nonly kernel effect is the FSM birth event: full_spec enters at plan (spec-first), fast_path enters at implement. An EXPLICIT theia run --from-plan … --track <t> overrides every task's track for\nthat invocation only — loud at start, audited on run.created (trackOverride), the approved artifact untouched:\nTrack When Pipeline\nfull-spec\nfeatures, contract/schema/auth changes, integrations, anything change-managed\nbrief → spec (plan DAG + acceptance criteria) → spec-approved gate → implement → verify → review → human merge\nfast-path\nsmall fixes with a repro and a check on the touched path, no API/schema/authz change\nDoR-lite → implement → verify → review → human merge\nTrack eligibility is checked mechanically where possible (diff touches\nschema/auth paths ⇒ full-spec forced — a path-set policy value), and the\nspec is a content-hashed contract: implement runs pin the spec digest they\nsatisfy, so a drifted spec fails the gate instead of silently diverging\n(claudexor INV-081).\n3. Gates — FSM states, not checklist rows Gate Enforcement\nDoR\ntask cannot leave draft without goal + track + acceptance criteria fields (schema-validated)\nspec-approved\nfull-spec tasks cannot enter implement without an approval event citing the spec digest; shipped as content-hashed plan artifacts — theia run --from-plan refuses any status but approved, every created task records the plan digest, and any edit (add-context) produces a new digest back in draft (INV-081)\nverify\nthe verifier's executed result is ground truth; fail overrides any claim and returns the task (A2)\nstop-and-fix\nN consecutive verifier failures (policy value, default 3) freezes the task to escalated — a human or the lead re-scopes; the frozen state carries the full evidence trail\nhuman merge\nprotected-branch merge and any production action are absent from kernel capabilities entirely ( 10-integrations.md §5 )\ndocs-DoD\ntask completion requires its artifact set (report, changelog entries) — presence-checked\npre-task\npreTask policy commands run before a task's first implement dispatch; exits + output digests recorded as evidence; a mustPass failure freezes the task for the lead without spending a model call — \"before starting work, do X\" as an interpreter, not prose\nEvery gate outcome is an event (auditable) and a metric (§ observability).\nASSUMPTION records are first-class rows with owners; an open assumption on a\nmerge-bound task blocks the gate — the competitor's rule, mechanized.\n4. Data classes and masking (the gateway gate)\nThree classes, assigned per source in.theia/config/ manifests and carried\non every fact, chunk, and artifact as a column:\nClass Examples Gateway behavior\nprohibited\nsecrets, keys, payment data, personal data without basis\ncall blocked ( fatal(policy) ); write path drops or quarantines; never leaves the process\nrestricted\ninternal code, non-public contracts, masked personal data\nmasked before the bytes leave (deterministic placeholders, reversible only via the local store); allowed to whitelisted transports\npermitted\npublic / de-identified content\nallowed + audited\nMasking happens at the ONE seam all calls cross ( 04-model-gateway.md §5 )\n— the property the competitor's playbook asks the engineer to remember.\nDetectors are shared between the write-path PII gate and the prompt gate\n(Luhn-checked cards, key/mnemonic patterns, document numbers), with the\nverbatim-substrate nuance kept: a chunk keeps the dialogue and loses only the\nsecret. Confidentiality filters at retrieval are default-deny — a caller\nfilter can narrow but never widen visibility.\n5. Policy values\nAll governance knobs are typed policy values ( packages/schema 's\npolicy module),\ndeclared in manifests, validated at load, and consumed by a named component —\nan unconsumed policy fails CI (staged-field rule, A3). The catalog covers:\nbudgets (cost/time/rounds per task class), delegation depth/fan-out,\ntrack-forcing path sets, tool grants per role (the MCP least-privilege\nmatrix as data), data-class assignments, retention windows, masking modes,\nuncertainty thresholds (calibrated, receipt-carrying — A11), pre-task\ncommand gates, context-injection scopes ( kind: Context ), pricing\noverlays with per-transport costBasis ( kind: Pricing ), and the\nfreshness-automation posture ( indexPolicy auto|alert|manual +\nbuild budget + serve cadence — alert-first, auto-spend by standing\nconsent only). The\ncompetitor's Policy Engine bullet list maps 1:1 onto this catalog; the\ndifference is that ours has an interpreter per row\n( 12-invariants.md pins the mapping).\n6. Vendor & model registry\nExternal transports (LLM providers, embedding APIs, MCP servers) register in\na manifest with: provider, service, data classes permitted, region, owner,\nlast-review date, exit plan — the DORA-shaped ICT registry as config the\ngateway enforces (an unregistered transport cannot be routed to), not a\nspreadsheet someone maintains.\n7. Incident path\nThe incident sequence is built from shipped mechanisms, operator-driven:\nanomaly events surface loudly (typed, counted, rendered ‼ in every view);\nthe guards contain the blast (freeze to escalated, budget pause,\nstop-and-fix) so failures are bounded and cheap; a stop is graceful and the\nlog IS the snapshot; theia diagnose runs the RCA over the audit trail and\nmemorizes the fingerprint; resume continues from the chain-intact prefix.\nEvery step is an event; the postmortem cites the trace. A severity policy\nthat auto-triggers the full sequence rides the daemon channel when it lands\n( 13-roadmap.md queued rows).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/10-integrations.html":{"title":"Integrations — harness sessions, MCP, tools, <code class=\"yfm-clipboard-inline-code\" role=\"button\" tabindex='0' id=\"inline-code-id-t0y2pl0a\">.theia/</code>, sandboxing","content":"1. The failure this closes\nA memory/orchestration platform that lives beside the engineer's actual tools\nis dead weight: the agent re-greps what the index knows, the session that\ncompacts loses the afternoon's execution state, and \"the platform\" becomes a\nsecond place to look. Aletheia therefore integrates in both directions — under Claude Code and Codex as their memory, map, and audit layer, and over them as the orchestrator that drives them as harnesses — with the\nend-user's own tools and MCP servers first-class inside Aletheia's runs.\n2. Under the harnesses — hooks and session continuity\nLifecycle hooks (agentmemory's proven surface): SessionStart injects\nthe project brief + relevant memory + codebase-model pointers; UserPromptSubmit recalls against the prompt; PostToolUse captures\nobservations (SHA-256 deduped, privacy-filtered); PreCompact runs the\nFocusMemory play — extract Σ before the harness's lossy compaction, with\nperiodic checkpoints so the loss bound is the checkpoint interval, not the\nwindow; Stop distills the session into factual/experiential memory.\nClaude Code gets the full set via.claude/settings.json; Codex gets it\nvia project.codex/hooks.json + [mcp_servers.theia] in project.codex/config.toml ( theia hooks install codex — probed at codex-cli\n0.151.0: the Claude-shaped hook schema, transcript_path on every\nevent). Two gates stand between install and dispatch, and the installer\nprints both loudly instead of letting silence be discovered: project\ntrust must be PERSISTED in the codex home config ( --trust appends it\nas an explicit opt-in; a -c override does not satisfy the gate), and\nnon-managed hooks are review-gated per hash ( /hooks interactively, or --dangerously-bypass-hook-trust for vetted automation). The\nagentmemory-era plugin-hooks path is retired ( plugin_hooks = removed\nat this CLI). SessionStart also reports index\nfreshness and, under indexPolicy: auto, kicks a detached budget-capped\nbuild; theia hooks install git adds the post-commit dirty-marks hint\n(zero model calls).\nSession resume rides each harness's native mechanism ( --resume, codex exec resume ) through the gateway's session support; lineage is\nverified by content hashing before resuming (meridian's pure lineage module — resuming the wrong conversation is a silent corruption class).\nMCP server ( theia serve ): the codebase-model query tools\n(find_symbol, call_graph, intent search), memory tools (recall, remember,\nas-of queries), run-audit readers, and run_diagnose — the diagnostician\nexposed to host models (post-hoc RCA over a stored run's log, sharing the\nCLI verb's fingerprint memory) — so any MCP-speaking host model\nreads the same truth the kernel does. Tool descriptions carry contracts\nonly; behavioral guidance for the calling model lives in server\ninstructions (the MCP discipline: never smuggle policy into tool\ndescriptions).\n3. Over the harnesses — tools inside Aletheia runs\nInside a kernel run the WORK tools are the harness's own (edit, shell,\napply_patch), governed by capability, not instruction: the sandbox tier\nbounds the filesystem/network, probed per-role gateway.harnessConfig knobs toggle harness features, and git/merge/deploy are absent from every\nagent's reach (the platform commits — THE-054). Platform-side execution —\nverify commands, pre-task gates, declared commits — produces evidence\nobjects: exit codes, artifact digests, byte counts, never prose summaries\n(A12), and runs in the task's own workspace. The user's OWN MCP servers\ncompose at the host-session layer (their Claude/Codex config, beside our\nhooks) and can be declared into kernel runs through the same probed\nharness-config seam; a first-class per-run user-MCP manifest graduates\nwith a consumer ( 13-roadmap.md container-provider\nrow).\nThe agent-side MCP belt. Kernel-driven codex invocations carry\ntheia's OWN MCP server as per-invocation config ( -c mcp_servers.theia…,\nnever a global config write): theia serve on stdio against the same\nstore (explicit --home — never cwd discovery from inside a workspace\ncopy), the least-privilege read-mostly tool set ( run.mcpBelt.tools policy value; memory_add / run_* / run_check opt-in — run_check executes ONE of the task's own verify commands host-side in the task workspace, a closed list at spawn: the sandbox-blocked hypothesis loop without widening the sandbox, the verifier keeping the verdict), and belt writes stamped run:<id>/task:<id>. Read tools advertise readOnlyHint and the server\nsets default_tools_approval_mode=auto — the probed combination that\nauto-approves reads under exec's approval_policy=never while write\ntools stay approval-gated. The belt is excluded from the replay key (its\nresults fold into recorded completions); a user session's own theia MCP\ncomposes independently on the same WAL store. This is the AOCI hybrid:\nthe map injected underneath, on-demand queries on top.\nPlane comparison — who owns which tool (Q7). The harness edits; theia\nknows, remembers, audits, verifies. They compose, never compete:\nPlane theia (MCP belt / platform) Claude Code built-ins codex built-ins\nRepo map & symbols\ncodebase_blueprint/search/symbol (precomputed, deterministic)\nGlob/Grep/Read (exploratory)\nshell grep/cat (exploratory)\nProject memory\nmemory_search/add/tree (persistent, temporal)\nsession context only\nsession context + own memories dir\nEditing & execution\n— (platform commits; verify commands)\nEdit/Write/Bash\napply_patch/shell\nAudit & RCA\nrun_audit, run_diagnose (hash-chained log)\n—\n—\nWeb\npolicy-gated ( web_search harness knob)\nWebSearch/WebFetch\nweb_search tool\nGit / merge / deploy\nplatform-only, capability-absent for agents (THE-054)\nuser-mediated\nsandbox-blocked\nHarness tool governance (the P10 ladder, probed). (1) capability\nabsence first: sandbox tier + jail govern fs/network; (2) config\nwhitelist per role ( gateway.harnessConfig.<role> policy value) — every\nkey PROBED on the pinned CLI before trust: web_search=\"disabled\" verifiably removes the tool; the plausible tools.web_search=false is\naccepted-but-ineffective at codex-cli 0.151.0, and unknown -c keys pass\nsilently, so acceptance is never evidence (ledger §4); (3) prompts last,\nnever as enforcement.\n4..theia/ — the on-disk contract\n.theia/ config/ YAML manifests (apiVersion/kind) — user-versioned prompts/ per-role prompt extensions — user-versioned LEAD.md REVIEWER.md … appended to baked-in role prompts PROMPTS.md or one file, \"### ROLE\" sections objects/ content-addressed blobs (sha256/…) store.db SQLite SoT (events, facts, entries, generations, heads) index/ generation-named FAISS/BM25 shards runs/<run-id>/ per-run worktree refs, artifacts, REPORT.md\nPrompts are the user's (overseer's override mechanism, adopted\nverbatim): baked defaults + appended per-role overrides, tracked by the user's repository VCS; every agent run's assembled prompt is recorded by\ndigest, so \"what did the model actually see\" is answerable forever.\nConfig is declarative YAML, closed kinds, validated on load\n( 01-architecture.md §6 ). Unknown kinds and\nunconsumed knobs fail loudly (P10, staged-field rule). kind: Context carries user rules and reference material injected\ninto agent prompts as content — each entry names a source (inline /\npath / glob), a scope ( always, or roles[] / tracks[] / pathTriggers[] — globs matched against the task diff where committed\nwork exists, desc-paths otherwise), and a char budget; what a run\ninjected is recorded as {entry, digest} on its audit trail. \"Read docs\nat X\" becomes the docs arriving, not an instruction the model may skip. Rules files compose in three modes (Q8): native passthrough —\nworkspace copies preserve the repo, so codex reads AGENTS.md exactly as\nanywhere (unbudgeted, unaudited, harness-specific); audited injection — a Context manifest globs any rules file (.ai/*,.cursor/rules,\nAGENTS.md) into scoped, budgeted, digest-recorded blocks — the layer\nnative reads cannot be; rules-SoT ( run.rulesSoT policy value) makes\nthe manifests the ONLY path: layer 1 disables the native read\nper-invocation ( project_doc_max_bytes=0, sentinel-probed), layer 2\nmasks in-repo rules files at workspace copy (tracked → sentinel +\nskip-worktree so the commit sweep can never land the masking; untracked\n→ removed), layer 3 (jail bind-masking over global files) arrives with\nthe jailed tiers. Default guidance: point manifests at NON-native files\nand house policy; leave AGENTS.md to the native read — or enable\nrulesSoT when audit outweighs the duplicate tokens.\nEverything content-dependent is hash-addressed — change detection,\ncache validity, and audit all reduce to digest comparison (A24).\nFUSE-ready by layout: stable paths, enumerable directories,\none-fact-per-file where a human would grep; the future FUSE mount\n( 13-roadmap.md ) adds virtual read views (rendered\nmemory cards, run timelines) without changing the physical layout.\nMulti-root workspaces: a kind: Workspace manifest declares\nnamed repo roots sharing this ONE.theia/ store —\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Workspace metadata: { name: shop } spec: roots: - { name: api, path:../shop-api } - { name: web, path:../shop-web } primary: api\nDiscovery walks UP from cwd to the nearest.theia/ with a store — store.db is the home marker; intent entries key rootName/relpath; memory recall unions the workspace scope with every\nroot scope; no manifest = one implicit root, byte-identical to before.\nPer-root overlays (closest-scope-first): a declared root may\ncarry its own.theia/{prompts,config} INSIDE the root repo — that\nrepo's team versions its overrides beside the code. A store-less.theia/ is exactly this overlay (the home-marker rule keeps discovery\nfrom mistaking it for a workspace); a root with a FULL.theia is its\nown workspace when invoked from inside — closest scope first at the\ninvocation level too. The precedence law, per scope kind (root >\nworkspace > baked, pinned by test): prompts append outermost-first\nso the closest scope has the last word; policy merges key-wise, and\noverlays accept ONLY the keys with per-root consumers — gateway.harnessConfig (knob-wise per role) and run.preTask (a\nroot's list REPLACES the workspace list whole-array) — anything else\nfails loudly naming the workspace home (no accepted-but-dead knobs,\nTHE-051); context entries union, root entries shadowing name\ncollisions, sources resolving against the root's path, provenance on\nthe audit event. Recorded prompt digests keep saying what each role\nactually saw, now per root.\n5. Git discipline\nEach implementer task runs in an isolated workspace on an ai/* branch\n(ralphex/overseer + the competitor's least-privilege matrix). Workspaces are copies of the root tree by default: copies carry the untracked\ndependencies — node_modules,.env, build caches — so verify commands run\nwithout reinstall; the pre-existing untracked set is snapshotted into the\nworkspace's git excludes so a dependency can never enter task history, and\nthe recorded base sha (run event + workspace config) anchors what counts as\nthe task's own work. workspaceMode: clone remains the escape hatch.\nAgents edit files; the platform commits — in the order an implementer declares ( commits: [{message, paths}] ) with a sweep commit for the\nremainder — and trunk is written only by the merger's --no-ff step. The merge target is a per-run branch (single-root git runs): theia run creates a git worktree at.theia/runs/<id>/trunk on theia/run-<suffix> (override --branch ), cut from the current branch's\nupstream — else origin/HEAD, else local HEAD, said honestly ( --base overrides) — so a run never bases on a stale or dirty checkout and never\nwrites the operator's branch; the finished branch is the deliverable (merge\nit or open a PR). A branch-name conflict fails loudly naming the fix; task\nworkspaces still copy the home repo (deps arrive) but branch from the run\nbranch TIP, so dependents see producer merges; resume re-attaches a pruned\ntrunk to the recorded branch. --no-run-branch keeps in-checkout\nintegration (the swebl S1 driver's posture — its patch collection diffs the\nworkspace tree); multi-root runs and forks keep their existing semantics.\nSandbox posture is the sandboxTier policy value: standard (workspace-write), jailed-full (danger-full-access INSIDE theia jail —\nprobed at run start, refuses when the jail is unavailable), trusted-full (full access unjailed — a loud, manifest-recorded opt-in). Protected\nbranches and any production surface\nare human-gate territory — the kernel is incapable of merging them, not\nmerely instructed (the capability is absent from its tool grants, P10).\nCross-root work is ONE run: tasks carry a root label; a task may\ndeclare roots: [primary, …] for a layout-preserving multi-root workspace\n(one copy per root under ws-<task>/<rootName>/, commits in the primary,\nverify commands see sibling roots at relative paths); a producer's landed\nmerge re-indexes its root before consumers dispatch, so the new contract\npropagates by mechanism; sibling merges hold at a barrier and land\ntogether, recorded as ONE run.changeset (shas + plan digest) — and a\nbreak reverts or freezes per policy with the half-landed set named,\nbecause git has no cross-repo atomicity and the run never pretends at 2PC.\n6. Sandboxing — theia jail (shipped; platform-dispatched)\nThe jail is a platform-dispatched seam with one contract (notes/23):\nthe workload runs with full capability inside a boundary the platform\nowns — the workspace and named state dirs writable, the rest of the\nuser's data not, the network shared. Backends: bwrap on linux (below,\nunchanged), an AppContainer launcher on win32 (low-box token,\nper-path icacls grants, internetClient + privateNetworkClientServer\ncapabilities, inherited stdio — embedded PowerShell/C# core in appcontainer.ts; loopback stays denied by OS design, which is fine\nbecause the MCP belt rides stdio), and none yet on darwin — the probe\nrefuses naming the queued container backend (docs/13). probeJail and\nthe doctor line select per platform; the tier vocabulary is unchanged\n(tiers are intent, backends are implementation).\nOn linux the backend is bubblewrap: a\nuser+mount-namespace sandbox with a read-only host root, fresh /dev + /proc, and an explicit RW allowlist composed at call time — theia jail [--rw=PATH]... -- <cmd> is the standalone primitive, and the jailed-full tier wraps every codex spawn in it (RW = the task workspace + ~/.codex + tmp) through an opaque spawn-wrapper seam the adapter never\nsees (translational adapters, THE-011). Availability is probed, never\nassumed (THE-013): theia doctor runs a live no-op inside a fresh\nsandbox, and the tier gate re-probes at run start — no bwrap or no user\nnamespaces means a loud refusal, never a silent degrade. The network stays\nshared by design (the jailed workload is a harness that must reach its\nprovider); --die-with-parent rides every invocation as the containment\nstand-in. Missing RW grants refuse at compose time — a silently skipped\ngrant would resurface as an opaque EROFS deep inside the workload, the\nexact failure class the ledger §4 sandbox saga records. Deliberately still\ndeferred: the prctl TRUE subreaper (adopting double-fork escapes reparented\nto init — needs a native call Node lacks; the jail's --die-with-parent remains their only cover), a per-harness RW path registry beyond codex, and\nan --unshare-net policy value. The registry-groups + /proc-descendant\nreap now runs on EVERY run/TUI exit path ( reaper.ts — the sweep half of\nthe old deferral, landed).\nResource containment (the layer the jail cannot be). bubblewrap is a\nmount/user-namespace tool with zero cgroup capability — isolation and\nresource control are different primitives that COMPOSE as prefixes. The run.resources policy ( {memoryMax, cpuQuota, tasksMax}, docs/15 §3)\nruns every kernel-driven harness spawn, host verify command, and pre-task\ngate inside a systemd-run --user --scope under theia-run.slice, the\nscope wrapping OUTSIDE the jail so the whole sandboxed tree is capped; MemorySwapMax=0 rides every memory cap by law (a hog that merely spills\ninto swap thrashes the machine — the measured failure the caps exist to\nclose). Unset limits are the identity wrap (byte-identical spawns);\nconfigured-but-unprobeable refuses the run loudly (THE-013). win32's\nanalogue (Job Objects on the AppContainer launcher) is named deferred.\nThe container backend ( run.jail.backend: container ). podman-or-docker\n(probed in that order) as the jail primitive: the host is simply absent\nfrom the container fs (RW grants become bind mounts, the cwd always\nmounted), the network stays shared per the jail contract, and the run.resources caps map onto the engine's native flags — one primitive\ncarrying both halves, and the first backend available on every platform\nwith an engine (linux, darwin — which has no native backend — win32 via\nDocker Desktop). The image ( run.jail.image ) is an explicit operator\nchoice, never defaulted; the probe demands it locally ( --pull=never ),\nrefusing by naming the pull. Harness-inside-container (a codex-bearing\nimage) is the named next train; today the arm carries command workloads\n( theia jail --backend container, jailed verify).\n7. Claude Code as the control surface — the store as the control bus\nThe slide-12/14 contract made mechanism: a host model session (Claude\nCode first; any MCP host) PLANS, STARTS, WATCHES, STEERS, QUESTIONS and\nANSWERS any number of theia runs — with no daemon. Three pieces:\nThe bus is the run's own log. Control appended by ANY process on\nthe WAL store — theia msg, theia answer, the MCP control tools —\nrides the same hash-chained scope as the run's events (appends are\nchain-safe cross-process), and the LIVE run folds it at its dispatch\nboundaries ( ControlPoll, consumed at the top of every coordinator\nround): a user.note folds into Σ guidance with the exact live-msg\nsemantics, a run.stop_request lands as the graceful resumable pause.\nReplay needs nothing special — the bus IS the log.\nQuestions route into the session ( run.interaction: bus, or --interaction bus; run_start spawns runs with it). A clarify\nbecomes a run.question event and the run WAITS (bounded, run.interactionWaitSeconds ) for a run.answer appended by the host\nsession's run_answer tool or the theia answer CLI; timeout is the\nexisting honest freeze (escalated, resumable), and a LATE answer\nfolds into guidance so it never rots on the log. The TTY forms (TUI\nquestion-by-question, readline) are untouched — the bus arm is for\nruns a session hosts.\nThe control tools are a store reader + a supervisor of CLI verbs ( serve-control.ts; registered on the user-facing theia serve —\nnever on the belt, whose --tools filter excludes them): reads\n( run_list/run_status/run_events/run_wait — long-poll, cursor by\nlastEventId) come straight off the log; actions either append control\nevents ( run_msg/run_answer/run_stop ) or spawn the EXISTING audited\nverb as its own process ( run_start → a detached theia run --interaction bus, plan_create → theia plan, run_probe → theia probe ), so every action carries exactly the audit trail a\nterminal operator's would. theia hooks install claude registers the\nserver in the project's.mcp.json and drops the /theia skill —\nthe operating flow with its HUMAN GATES (a plan is approved only\nafter the user saw it; the skill says so, the tool description says\nso, and the MCP instructions say so three times deliberately).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/14-tui.html":{"title":"TUI & CLI surfaces — the run tree, the views, the home screen","content":"1. The failure this closes\nA live agent run rendered as a scrolling transcript fails its operator three\nways at once: context-overload blindness (a verifier's output floods\nscrollback and the one failing step is buried), spectator paralysis (you\nwatch a loop happen with no way to steer short of killing the run), and zero drill-down (per-task cost, verdict evidence, and anomaly payloads\nexist in the log but have no live surface). Aletheia's answer is the run\ntree: one live, navigable, collapsible tree of the run with a zoomable\ndetail viewport — the operational grammar of dagger's TUI, rendered entirely\nfrom the event log. The TUI is a fold with a cursor: seek, replay parity,\nand byte-pinned golden-frame tests come free because the view is a pure\nfunction of the log (THE-001 applied to a screen).\n2. Views and where they appear\nEvery run surface renders from the same fold; the views differ only in how\nmuch terminal they claim:\nView What it is For\n--tui\nthe run tree: live follow or stored-run browse with time-travel seek\nthe operator — the built-in default on an interactive terminal\n--cli\nredrawing task×phase board + cost ticker, no alternate screen\nglanceable progress in a scrollback-friendly form\n--plain\none line per event as it lands (phase/verdict/evidence-ref/cost; anomalies loud!! )\nscripting, CI, logs\nsummary\none-shot fold printed after the fact\ntheia show <runId> with no flag (a glance tool must not grab the terminal)\nPrecedence: flag > ui.defaultView policy value > built-in ( tui ).\nDegradation is honest and loud (THE-052): no stdout TTY, no usable stdin, or --non-interactive each degrade tui / cli to plain with a notice — a\nTUI in a pipe is garbage and is never silently attempted. A piped brief\n( echo task | theia run ) still opens the TUI when stdout is a terminal: the\nbrief is read from the pipe, then key input reopens /dev/tty; no /dev/tty (CI) degrades loudly. theia replay never renders — it is the\ndeterminism gate, not a viewer.\n3. Layout — three zones + one viewport\n⣽ run run2ak7… · fast_path · $0.0312/$5.00 · 14 calls · 96 events · 2m11s running tasks: 1 running · 1 done · 0 pending · anomalies 0 [@ event 96/96 · live]\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ▼ ✔ tsktm7… [fast_path] add --stats flag merged r4 $0.0212 ├╴ ✔ r1 implement implementer → ready 2 commits, 2 evidence 4.1s ├╴ ✔ r2 verify verifier → verify_pass test_run pnpm test 1.2s ├╴ ✔ r3 review reviewer → approve 3.4s ╰╴ ✔ r4 merge merger → merged sha 1a2b3c4 0.6s ▼ ⣽ tskaa1… [full_spec] migrate the api implement r2 $0.0100 ├╴ ✔ r1 plan planner → clarify 2 questions answered 8.0s ╰╴ ⣽ r2 implement implementer codex call live… 41s ▶ ◌ tskbb2… [fast_path] update the consumers blocked on tskaa1…\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── verdict tskaa1… r1 · planner → clarify Q: which auth scheme does the new api keep? → \"oauth, drop keys\" Q: is the v1 endpoint retired in this change? → \"yes\" evidence: — note: \"ambiguous brief: two auth paths in the codebase\"\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • m steer • p prompt w wrap • v view • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q stop\nThe figure, walked: the header (2 lines) carries the run glyph (⣽\nlive / ✔ completed / ✖ failed / ‖ paused), the FULL run id, track, the cost\nticker against budget, call/event counts, elapsed, a worded task tally, the\nanomaly count, warning hints ( ctx ⚠N context alerts, idx ⚠N stale-index\nentries), and the seek position ( live when following the head). The tree is the core: per-row STATE column, box-drawing rails ( ├╴ ╰╴ │ ),\nidentity + right-aligned facts (phase, rounds, per-task cost, dim duration —\nbright while running). The detail viewport (bottom split, per-node zoom)\nshows the selected node's full story — here a clarify verdict with its Q&A.\nThe keybar is one plain line (bold key + dim label, • separators, no\nreverse video; wrapped here for page width); it always tells the truth —\nkeys that do not apply in the current mode are omitted ( m / p only on a\nlive run, [ ] seek only in browse), and extras drop by fitting tier on\nnarrow terminals. Bindings without a bar slot ( L / H, F, g / G, the\nseek steps) all live in the? help overlay.\nThe node hierarchy (all of it a pure fold over the log)\nrun\n├── task* task.created / task.phase / task.verdict\n│ ├── round* one node per verdict (phase, role → outcome)\n│ │ ├── gateway call* gateway.call (task-attributed)\n│ │ └── tool call* tool.result leaves (✎ edit · $ command · ⚙ mcp)\n│ ├── evidence drawer verdict evidence[] as expandable leaves\n│ ├── note* workspace base, injected context, pre-task gates\n│ └── (blocked-on line) unmet deps, rendered inline\n├── anomaly* always top-level too — never buried\n├── steering note* user.note (msg / clarify answers)\n└── changeset / fork lineage run.changeset, run.forked (+ children)\nGlyph vocabulary (one meaning each; NO_COLOR keeps plain-text\nequivalents for every glyph): ✔ done-good (merged/pass/approve) · ✖ failed/discard · ◆ frozen (escalated, clarify-unanswered) · braille\nspinner ⣷⣯⣟⡿⢿⣻⣽⣾ in flight · ◌ pending/blocked · ‼ anomaly · » steering · ▼ / ▶ fold state · cursor = full-row background band ( ❯ under NO_COLOR). Dagger's law is kept: success condenses, failure flags\nitself — done-good tasks auto-collapse to one row; a task entering escalated or producing ✖ / ‼ auto-expands and the follow cursor jumps\nto it.\n4. Interaction\nNavigation (both modes):\nKey Action\n↑ / ↓ ( k / j )\nmove the cursor over visible nodes\n→ / ← ( l / h )\nexpand / collapse; ← on a leaf jumps to its parent\nL / H\nexpand / collapse every fold at once (a 9-round task must not cost 9 keystrokes to open)\nEnter\nzoom: open/close the detail viewport for the cursor node\nTab\ncycle focus tree ⇄ detail; PgUp / PgDn page the FOCUSED pane\ng / G\nfirst / last node\ne\nhop to the next ✖ / ◆ / ‼ — the diagnostic path\nf\nfollow mode: cursor tracks the newest activity; any manual move turns it off (live starts on)\nF\nfollow depth: cycle task / round / tool — pick your altitude\n+ / -\nverbosity 0–2: 0 condenses to task/round headers (anomalies and steering always render — honesty is not a tier), 1 = the standard tree, 2 reveals dim inline detail lines with [0.07s] durations; the live activity pane scales with it (2/6/10 rows). Composes with F: F says where the cursor follows, verbosity says what renders. The fold posture (verbosity + follow depth) PERSISTS across runs ( ~/.config/theia/tui-state.json, fail-open)\nw\nwrap the cursored row's full label across hang-indented lines\nv\nopen the node's full command/output body in $EDITOR (from local content-addressed blobs; bodies never ride events or telemetry — THE-055)\n?\nhelp overlay (every binding + the glyph legend)\nq\nleave: browse = quit; live = graceful stop (below); ended hold = quit\nText boxes (steer, clarify answers, the home task box) share one line\neditor: cursor keys, Home/End, Ctrl-A/E, word hops (Ctrl-←/→), Ctrl-W/U/K\nkills, Delete — and bracketed paste delivers a paste as ONE insertion, so\na pasted newline inserts instead of submitting and a pasted q cannot\nquit. The caret renders in-text ( ▏ ) and BLINKS while the box owns the\nkeys (static under --no-spinner — monitors must not see frame churn). Tab hops message ⇄ tree mid-draft: navigate and inspect the run\nwithout losing the composed text (the dimmed draft names the way back, m re-focuses it; q /Ctrl-C still stop — stopping outranks a draft).\nWith the detail viewport open, both separators NAME their pane and mark\nthe focused one ( ──┤ tree · focus ├── ).\nIn-flight activity. From the moment a dispatch's model call starts, a\ndim activity line shows who is in flight ( ⋯ implementer tsk…x (model) call in flight… · 4m32s [0 tools] ), enriched per tool completion — a\nlong tool-less call is never a bare spinner. Under it, a rolling tail of\nthe dispatch's recent tools (the live commands LOG, 8 kept, newest first;\nthe row cap follows verbosity). A backing-off call names its wait: ↻ retry 2/4 in 4s — <classified reason> — a 429/500/session-limit loop\nnever reads as a stuck session. Anomaly rows fold consecutively-identical\nrepeats into one ×N row (the header count keeps the truth), and event\nbursts refold at repaint cadence — keys, q and Ctrl-C included, stay\nresponsive under spam. p opens the in-flight\ncall's PROMPT (a pre-call exchange blob, class-gated like the audit\nblob): the operator reads what a long-running block is running WITH, not\nonly after it lands. THEIA_LOG_FILE=<path> appends every event's --plain line PLUS its full JSON payload (max verbosity — nothing the\none-line render truncated is lost; payloads carry digests, never bodies)\nand the live feed lines — tail it from a second terminal for parallel\nanalysis.\nExit is clean. Every run/TUI exit path — finished, hard stop, ended\nhold — sweeps children: registered harness process groups first, then a\n/proc descendant reap (TERM, short grace, KILL) for strays the registries\nnever saw. theia run --abandon <runId> is the operator's kill for a\npaused/killed run that will not be resumed: it appends the terminal run.finished {halted: abandoned}, after which theia prune reclaims it.\nEnded hold. A finished run — discards included — keeps the screen with\nan ■ run ended — … banner: browse, seek ( [ ] ), and v stay live for\ninspection; q leaves (the hold is skipped when the operator had already\npressed q ). The summary tail still prints to normal scrollback after.\nLive-run verbs (each an existing kernel mechanism, only the surface is\nthe TUI):\nSteer — m. A one-line footer input; the note lands as an audited user.note in the hash-chained log AND folds into the live coordinator's\nΣ guidance immediately. With the cursor on a task node, m targets THAT\ntask by default (the input label names the addressee: steer → tsk… / steer → run ); a note to an already-terminal task reroutes to run-level\nguidance carrying the original addressee — a message is never silently\ndropped.\nAnswer — clarify forms. When a role returns clarify, the detail\nviewport becomes the question form (one question at a time, the why shown dim). Answers fold into Σ and become memory facts (never asked\ntwice); Esc declines the remainder and the task freezes ◆ escalated exactly as --non-interactive would.\nStop — q / Ctrl+C. First press: graceful — header flips to ‖ stopping, in-flight verdicts land, the run pauses resumable, the\nresume hint prints after screen teardown. Second press: hard stop —\nharness process groups killed, exit 130. One two-stage law, every\nsurface.\nBrowse-mode seek ( theia show <runId> --tui ): [ / ] step the event\ncursor ±1, { / } ±10, Home / End (or 0 / $ ) genesis/head — the tree re-folds from\nthe event prefix, so \"what did the run look like the moment the reviewer\nbounced it\" is two keystrokes. The header shows @ event k/N; a verdict\nnode's detail carries the copyable theia fork <run> --at <evt> command —\nfork points are verdict boundaries, and the TUI hands you the command rather\nthan spawning runs.\n5. The home screen\nBare theia on a two-way terminal opens the launcher: the doctor summary\n(same truth the verb prints), the index-freshness line (zero model calls),\nthe last five runs with lineage-honest rows (probe/ask scopes render as\nterminal · rows; forks wear forked·<outcome> ) and their LOCKED costs\nread from gateway events (never recomputed — THE-015), and a task input box\n— Enter hands the terminal to run --tui with the typed brief.? toggles help; non-TTY prints the help text unchanged. Briefs everywhere\n( run, plan, msg, probe, the home box) accept @path inclusions\nthrough the audited context path: read + digest + data-class gate, inlined\nas digest-headed blocks so the persisted brief pins the exact bytes ( @@ escapes a literal @; missing files refuse loudly).\n6. Honest states (THE-052 applied to a screen)\nTerminals below 20×5 get a one-line guard frame; anything larger renders\nwith hard horizontal clipping — every line truncates at the real column\ncount, progress stays legible in tiny panes, no reflow corruption.\nA run with only run.created renders the header + \"no tasks yet\"; a\nbroken hash chain shows ‼ audit chain BROKEN at <id> in the header while\nthe fold still renders the prefix — inspection is how tampering is\ndiagnosed.\nUnknown event types render as dim rows — never dropped, never a crash (the\nregistry can grow ahead of the viewer).\nAlternate screen + raw mode are restored on every exit path (finally +\nprocess exit hook + uncaught) — a crashed TUI never leaves the operator's\nterminal raw.\n7. Mechanics — why this is testable\nPure core, thin shell. Tree fold (events → model), keymap\n((state, key) → state + effect), and frame render ((model, state,\ncols×rows) → styled lines) are pure functions; the screen driver\n(alternate buffer, raw input decode incl. CSI sequences, resize, repaint)\nis the only I/O. Golden-frame tests pin rendered frames byte-for-byte at\nfixed sizes; keymap tests walk the reducer; seek tests assert\nprefix-fold ≡ fromEvents equality.\nDiff repaint. Only changed lines repaint (a shrinking frame clears\nits orphaned rows), coalesced ≥80ms; the spinner ticks on its own 120ms\ntimer in live mode only.\nDurations are event-derived. Per-row durations compute from event-id\nHLC wall time — rounds span verdict boundaries, tasks span\ncreated→last — so stored frames are deterministic; only live rows read\nthe injected clock. Sub-0.1s spans are suppressed.\nNo new dependencies. Node + ANSI, hand-rolled driver — the TUI stays\non the tested-artifact path and off the native-deps pothole list.\nLive wiring is one more onEvent sink on the tee store, plus a\ncoordinator note hook for steering and an interaction impl for clarify —\nno kernel restructuring; steering folded live is replay-equal to the\nsame log rehydrated (test-pinned).\n8. Out of scope (named, not silent)\nIn-TUI file-diff rendering (evidence is digest-referenced; commit evidence\nshows a git show <sha> hint — a workspace-read seam the browse mode does\nnot grow); contextual rollback (that is fork, and the TUI hands you the\ncommand); cross-process live attach of msg to a running TUI (arrives with\na daemon channel; today cross-process notes fold on the next rehydration,\nand the CLI prints that hint honestly).\nStatic rendering\n--no-spinner (or THEIA_NO_SPINNER=1 ) replaces the braille spinner and\nrunning-glyph animation with static ● glyphs — for screen readers, tmux\nframe captures, and line-diff monitors that must not see a change per\nanimation tick.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/15-configuration.html":{"title":"Configuration reference — manifests, policy values, prompts","content":"The one card an operator needs to configure Aletheia: every manifest kind\nwith a worked example, the full policy-value catalog with defaults and\nconsumers, the prompt-override mechanism, and the per-user config. Design\nrationale lives in 10-integrations.md §4 (layout, rules composition, overlays) and 11-governance.md §5 (why every knob names a\nconsumer); this doc is the reference. A knob listed here without a\nconsumer would fail CI (THE-051) — the catalog cannot drift from the code\nbecause the code is where it is parsed.\n1. Where configuration lives, and which scope wins Scope Location Versioned by\nuser\nthe platform user config dir ( @theia/user-dirs, env-paths semantics): linux ~/.config/theia/, Windows %APPDATA%\\dokimelabs\\theia\\, macOS ~/Library/Application Support/theia/ — XDG_* overrides win everywhere, and an existing legacy ~/.config/theia keeps winning on win32/darwin. config.yaml — the GitHub PAT for release download/tracking, openai.apiKey for the openai embedding posture only ( OPENAI_API_KEY env wins; the default local embedder needs no key — its models live in ~/.cache/theia/models, THEIA_MODELS_DIR overrides), dev.root for --dev, and update.channel (release | prerelease — the developer opt-in, never installer-written; 16-release.md §5 ). Plus ~/.config/theia/config/*.yaml manifests (the shared-store train): Policy / Pricing / TransportRegistry apply to EVERY workspace as the outermost scope — Workspace and Context kinds refuse loudly there (a user-scope Workspace would re-root every project; Context sources have no root to resolve against)\nthe user's dotfiles\nworkspace\n.theia/config/*.yaml manifests +.theia/prompts/\nthe project repo\nroot overlay\n<root>/.theia/{config,prompts} inside a declared multi-root member\nthat root's own repo\nbaked\ncode defaults (the config of record — a bare theia init runs the measured stack)\ntheia-dev\nStore placement ( theia init ) is a first-class choice, never an\naccident of cwd: inside a git working tree (linked worktrees included)\nthe store defaults to the worktree root — one store per checkout,\nhowever deep the invoking shell, so sibling terminals share one index\ninstead of minting nested stores that re-index the same content; outside\nany repo the default is a user-home store ( ~/.local/share/theia/stores/<slug>-<hash>, registered to the folder in ~/.config/theia/workspaces.yaml ) — the folder stays clean; and theia init --here is the detached escape hatch that pins the store to\nthe invoking folder verbatim. Init prints the placement and the\nalternative every time.\nDiscovery walks UP from cwd checking, per directory, a local.theia/ with a store first ( store.db is the home marker — an overlay-only.theia/ can never be mistaken for a workspace home), then a workspaces.yaml registry mapping for that directory (a row whose store\nlost its store.db is skipped with a notice). Precedence is closest\nscope first, per scope kind: prompts append outermost-first so the\nclosest scope has the last word; policy merges key-wise across ALL\nfour scopes — defaults < user-home manifests < workspace < root overlay\n(root overlays accept only the keys with per-root consumers — gateway.harnessConfig knob-wise per role, and run.preTask, which a\nroot REPLACES whole-array; anything else fails loudly naming the\nworkspace home); context entries union, root entries shadowing name\ncollisions, sources resolving against the root's path.\nRecorded prompt digests always say what each role actually saw, per root.\n2. Manifest kinds\nAll manifests are k8s-shaped YAML ( apiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1, closed kind set, strict fields — an unknown kind or field refuses loudly, never\nhalf-applies).\nkind: Policy — the governance knobs\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: default } spec: run: maxUsdPerRun: 5 sandboxTier: standard # standard | jailed-full | trusted-full preTask: - { run: \"pnpm lint\", mustPass: true } gateway: routing: { reviewer: claude } # role → transport models: { reviewer: claude-opus-5 } ui: { defaultView: tui } maintenance: { indexPolicy: alert } memory: { analyzer: snowball, sentenceGraph: off } # plain = tantivy's default tokenizer; h0|h1 = the sentence lane\nkind: Workspace — multi-root declaration\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Workspace metadata: { name: shop } spec: roots: - { name: api, path:../shop-api } - { name: web, path:../shop-web } primary: api\nOne.theia/ store serves every root (root-qualified index entries, union\nrecall); a cross-repo task is ONE run. Absent manifest = one implicit root,\nbyte-identical behavior.\nkind: Context — rules and reference material as injected content\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Context metadata: { name: team-rules } spec: entries: - name: house-style source: { path: docs/CONVENTIONS.md } # or inline: / glob: inject: { roles: [ implementer, reviewer ] } # or 'always' / tracks / pathTriggers - name: cursor-rules source: { glob: \".cursor/rules/*\" } inject: always budgetChars: 4000 # optional; absent ⇒ FULL content loads\nEntries arrive in prompts as digest-headed content blocks; what a run\ninjected is {entry, digest} on its audit trail. pathTriggers globs match\nthe task's diff where committed work exists, description paths otherwise.\nAn explicit budgetChars is the ONLY trim; totals above run.contextAlertChars alert loudly while everything still loads\n(visibility over paternalism — see the catalog). Rules-file composition\nmodes (native passthrough vs audited injection vs rulesSoT ) are the 10-integrations.md §4 contract; theia init seeds this manifest from the rules files it detects\n( §5 ).\nkind: Pricing — rates without code\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Pricing metadata: { name: rates } spec: models: codex-terra: { inPerMTok: 2, outPerMTok: 12 } transports: codex: { costBasis: actual } # 'actual' overrides adapter figures — # a subscription seat may declare 0\nEvery gateway call records its priceSource ( adapter | manifest | embedded ); locked costs never recompute (THE-015).\nkind: TransportRegistry — the vendor registry the gateway enforces\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: TransportRegistry metadata: { name: vendors } spec: transports: - { name: codex-full, provider: openai, dataClasses: [ restricted, permitted ], owner: platform-team }\nAn unregistered transport cannot be routed to; trusted-full sandbox\nrouting additionally demands the registry row — the loud, double-keyed\nopt-in.\n3. The policy-value catalog\nDefaults are the config of record; every row names its consumer.\nValue Default Consumer / meaning\nrun.maxRoundsPerTask\n10\nkernel round budget per task\nrun.stopAndFixAfterFailures\n3\nconsecutive verifier failures → freeze to escalated\nrun.maxUsdPerRun\n10\ngateway budget metering AND the --budget default (explicit flag wins; 0 = unmetered); exhaustion pauses resumable ( --resume --budget N ); the ONE verdict repair may overdraft a bounded grace (notes/31)\nrun.maxDelegationDepth / maxDelegationFanout\n1 / 4\ndelegation bounds (THE-025)\nrun.freshPerspectiveAfter\n2\nimplement dispatches before the fresh-perspective flip\nrun.maxRescopes\n2\nlead rescopes that reset the round budget; past it, forced discard\nrun.stallPauseAfter\n3\nconsecutive no_progress outcomes on one task (gateway failures, off-contract replies, coerced illegal verdicts) pause the run resumable with one dispatch_stalled anomaly — the escalated↔no_progress cycle has no round-budget exit (the 47-lead-call live receipt); 0 disables\nrun.poolSizes\n{} (merger/lead pinned 1)\nrole pool sizes\nrun.workspaceMode\ncopy\ntask workspace mechanism ( copy carries untracked deps; clone the escape hatch)\nrun.sandboxTier\nstandard\nharness sandbox posture; jailed-full needs the probed jail, trusted-full needs the registry row\nrun.resources\n{} (uncapped)\nthe spawn-cap seam ( 10 §6 ): {memoryMax, cpuQuota, tasksMax} — every kernel harness spawn, host verify, and pre-task gate runs in a systemd-run --user --scope (slice theia-run.slice ) carrying the caps, so one hot tree cannot starve the machine. MemorySwapMax=0 rides every memory cap by law (without it the hog spills into swap and the box thrashes — the live 512M-under-128M receipt). Configured-but-unprobeable refuses the run loudly; the container jail backend enforces the same values natively ( --memory + --memory-swap, --cpus, --pids-limit )\nrun.jail.backend /.image\nauto / unset\nwhich sandbox primitive the jailed tier and theia jail use: auto = the platform native (bwrap / AppContainer; darwin has none — refuses), container = podman-or-docker (probed in that order) — isolation + native resource caps in one primitive, the backend that completes the platform matrix. image is required with container and is an explicit operator choice (a workload without its toolchain in the image fails honestly at the first command); the probe runs --pull=never, refusing a non-local image by naming the pull command\nrun.changesetPartialFailure\nrevert\nmulti-root changeset breakage: revert unwinds landed merges, freeze escalates naming the half-landed set\nrun.planVerifyPreflight\ndisable\nalways pre-flights each distinct authored verify (root, command) once at base on plan create ( 06-orchestration.md §7 ); opt-in for now — the pre-flight executes plan-authored commands before any approval exists, the same standing-consent bar as indexPolicy. Disabled with verifies present prints one hint line; the demo posture opts in\nrun.preTask[]\n[]\ncommands before a task's first implement dispatch; mustPass failure freezes without a model call\nrun.blueprintMinImportance\nunset\ncmd-run's blueprintSlice + plan/probe context collection: the injected blueprint's importance floor. Unset = the COMPLETE map (docs/05 §5 — the default posture); set, entries below the floor leave the injected map with a LOUD count line (never a silent trim), and the belt codebase_search / codebase_symbol tools still reach every entry. The operator's dose control where a complete multi-root map exceeds the model window (a 13-root production workspace rendered 3.8M chars ≈ 950K tokens); superseded by the task-scoped projection when it lands ( 13-roadmap.md )\nrun.contextAlertChars\n200 000\ninjected-context chars per job above which ONE loud alert fires (everything still loads; plan mode applies the same threshold to its collected context — run/plan parity)\nrun.contextRefuseChars\nunset\noptional hard line — freeze before dispatch, zero model calls, resumable\nrun.mcpBelt.enabled /.tools\ntrue / read-mostly set\ntheia's own MCP tools inside kernel-driven harness calls; memory_add / run_* opt-in\nrun.rulesSoT.enabled /.maskFiles\nfalse / AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md,.cursorrules,.cursor/rules,.ai\nmake Context manifests the ONLY rules path: native project-doc read disabled per-invocation + in-repo rules files masked at workspace copy\ngateway.routing\n{}\nrole → transport\ngateway.models\n{}\nrole → model id (resolved in the router; part of the replay key)\ngateway.maxRetries / retryBaseMs\n3 / 500\ncaller-owned retry over retryable results\ngateway.harnessConfig\n{}\nrole → probed per-invocation harness knobs (replay-key-bearing; unprobed keys are never trusted — acceptance is not evidence)\ngateway.maskOverrides\n{}\nthe class gate (docs/04 §5): per-detector re-classification — card_number: off | restricted | prohibited (closed detector names). Exists because high-recall detectors are wrong on some corpora by construction: a finance schema's 13–19-digit account IDs pass Luhn ~10% of the time and blocked theia plan on seeded dbo.* data (the 34938 report). A versioned governance act in the manifest — the diff shows it, counts stay on every audit event\ngateway.embeddings.provider\nlocal\nthe CLI's embedder resolver ( embeddings.ts ): which EmbeddingTransport mem reindex/search, index reindex/search, the serve belt, ask / probe and the memory bench construct — local (ONNX on this machine, zero USD) or openai (needs the key). Generation identity: vectors built by one triple are never queried by another ( 04 §4 )\ngateway.embeddings.model\nunset\nthe same resolver: a local registry id ( all-MiniLM-L6-v2, bge-small-en-v1.5 ) or an OpenAI model name; unset = the provider's default\nmemory.analyzer\nsnowball\nthe CLI's lexical resolver ( lexical.ts ): the analyzer mem reindex builds the BM25 lane with and every memory reader queries through — snowball ( @theia/text-analysis: per-token script + per-document language Snowball stemming, one function for index, query and scan lanes) or plain (tantivy's default tokenizer). Generation identity like the embedder: an analyzed generation is read only through its analyzer version, else plain with a notice ( 07 §3 )\nmemory.sentenceGraph\noff\nthe CLI's lexical resolver ( lexical.ts ): whether mem reindex builds the sentence tier into the generation ( h0 / h1, 18 §2.3 ) and how many hops the sentence lane expands at read ( h0 none, h1 one — h=2 drifts). Generation identity like the analyzer: the tier rides the builder version ( +sent@1 ); a reader asking for the lane on a generation without it gets no lane and a notice naming mem reindex. Off until the h=0 vs h=1 receipt (ledger §2)\nmemory.timeLane\ntrue\nthe event-time lane ( 07 §3/§6 ): when an ask names EXPLICIT absolute dates (regex only — an inferred range prunes on hallucinated windows), rows whose asserted [event_from, event_to) falls in the range join the retrieval union. On by its receipt (1 W / 0 L / 214 T paired, leak 0 — harmless where it cannot fire, decisive where it can)\ngateway.embeddings.autoDownload\ntrue\nthe local provider: a missing model fetches itself on first use (one notice line); false = offline posture — a missing model is a loud error naming theia setup --fetch-embeddings\nrun.interaction\nterminal\ncmd-run's interaction wiring (docs/10 §7): terminal = TTY forms (TUI/readline; non-interactive freezes visibly — unchanged); bus = clarifies become run.question events and the run waits for a cross-process run.answer ( run_answer MCP tool / theia answer ) — the arm run_start spawns runs with. --interaction overrides\nrun.interactionWaitSeconds\n600\nthe bus arm's bounded wait before the honest escalated freeze; a late answer still folds into guidance\nrun.sessionContinuity\noff\ncmd-run's runner wiring ( 04 §7, notes/15): off = cold dispatches; sigma-shared = cold dispatches whose ONLY cross-round shared state is the Σ-projected stable prompt prefix (stable-first ordering is unconditional; the mode arms the per-dispatch session_prefix digest audit). The accumulating resume mode was removed (ledger §4). --sessions / --no-sessions override per invocation. Off until its live receipt (ledger law)\nui.defaultView\ntui\nrun/show view when no flag is given\nmaintenance.indexPolicy\nalert\nauto builds stale entries within budget at run/plan start, post-merge, and on the serve loop; alert prints freshness lines only; manual silences even maintenance — the freshness LINES render under every policy\nmaintenance.indexBuildBudgetUsd\n1\ncap per auto-build wave; the stale remainder stays alerted\nmaintenance.serveRefreshSeconds\n300\nserve reconciler cadence (the check is a zero-model hash-diff)\nmaintenance.storeAlertGb\n5\nstore-size signal: when.theia (store + runs + objects + index) exceeds this, the run tail and doctor print a loud theia prune hint (kept run workspaces are full repo copies — the gauge is what makes the growth visible)\nmaintenance.indexCache\ntrue\nthe CLI's indexer construction ( index-cache.ts ): intent entries shared ACROSS workspaces via a user-level content-addressed cache ( ~/.cache/theia/index-cache, THEIA_INDEX_CACHE_DIR overrides) — a fresh.theia on already-indexed content builds at zero model cost (the 5-hour-quota receipt). Key pins builder+prompt version + path + content hash, so upgrades and edits self-invalidate; reads re-validate through the entry schema; every arm fails open. false = hermetic posture for measured eval arms. Sharing is WORKSPACE-SHAPE-scoped by construction: multi-root entries are root-qualified (paths and R-refs carry rootName/ ), so they reuse only in same-shaped workspaces — a single-root checkout of the same repo builds its own cache line (correct: its entries genuinely differ)\n4. Prompts\nRole prompts are baked defaults plus user overrides: drop LEAD.md, REVIEWER.md, … in.theia/prompts/ (or one PROMPTS.md with ### ROLE sections) and the content APPENDS to the baked prompt; per-root overlays\nappend after workspace overrides (closest scope last — the last word).\nVerdict vocabularies are enum-interpolated into the baked prompts, so a\nprompt cannot drift from the parser (drift-tested). Every assembled prompt\nis digest-recorded per job — \"what did the model actually see\" is always\nanswerable, per role, per root.\n5. Seeding and hooks\ntheia init [--harness codex|claude] [--embeddings local|openai] creates.theia/ with the default\nmanifests — routing seeded for the chosen harness family (unset: probed,\ncodex when both answer; both families are always vendor-registered so\nswitching later is one routing edit) — appends.gitignore, and detects existing AI-rules files (AGENTS.md,\nCLAUDE.md,.cursor/rules,.cursorrules,.ai/,.github/copilot-instructions.md; per root in multi-root workspaces):\nnon-native files are seeded into a generated kind: Context manifest\n( context-rules.yaml ) so they reach every harness and role — rule dirs\nas zero-match-tolerant globs, single files as path: entries; native\nfiles (AGENTS.md for codex, CLAUDE.md for Claude Code) are left to the\nharness's own read with the rulesSoT alternative named — printed,\nnever silent. Re-running init is merge-safe: seeded names are remembered\nin a sidecar, user edits survive verbatim, and an entry the user deleted\nstays deleted. theia doctor and both hook installers print the same\ncomposition summary (native · injected · undetected).\ntheia hooks install claude — memory + freshness hooks into.claude/settings.json (SessionStart context, prompt recall, PreCompact/\nStop checkpoints, statusline when absent); merge-idempotent, user entries\npreserved.\ntheia hooks install codex — project.codex/hooks.json + [mcp_servers.theia] in project.codex/config.toml; the two codex\ntrust gates print loudly on every install ( 10-integrations.md §2 ).\ntheia hooks install git — the post-commit dirty-marks hint (zero model\ncalls) feeding freshness.\ntheia setup --pat … --openai-key … --dev-root … --channel … — writes the user config ( --interactive runs the guided global setup); theia setup --fetch-embeddings [model] installs a local embedding model into the user cache ahead of first use (the doctor's embeddings line reports ready / not fetched / corrupt)\n( 16-release.md ).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/13-roadmap.html":{"title":"Roadmap — phases, exit criteria, deferred work","content":"Phasing follows one rule: each phase ships a vertically usable slice with\nits interpreters and receipts, never a horizontal layer cake. Deferral is\nclean (permanence discipline): a deferred item has concrete scope here, and\nthe shipped phases are self-consistent without it.\nPhase 0 — spine (foundations everything else stands on)\nScope: monorepo scaffold ( schema, terryid, event-log, store-sqlite, config, otel ); terryid + HLC with property tests mirroring the proofs;\nevent log with hash chain + replay; generations/heads + withTx + conformance\nsuites for the storage interfaces; YAML manifest loading; CI running\ntsc/tests/proofs/lints. Exit: THE-001…005 verified; replay-equality green on synthetic logs;\nproofs compile in CI.\nPhase 1 — gateway + fake harness\nScope: gateway engine with the seam vocabulary; harness-codex (subscription\nOAuth, exec/resume, stream parse, fault classify); harness-fake (record/replay + synthesized verdicts); embeddings-openai with\n(provider,model,dims) stamping; cost table + locked USD; data-class masking\nhooks (detectors shared with Phase 3's write gate); gateway OTel spans +\nmetrics. Exit: THE-010…016; golden wire fixtures per codex operation; a recorded\nsession replays byte-identically.\nPhase 2 — codebase model\nScope: tree-sitter AST facts + query tools; AOCI-shaped intent index with\nimportance-budgeted entries, hash-keyed incremental builds, generation\npublishing, reconciler; theia index; MCP exposure of the query tools;\ncodebase-QA benchmark harness (Where/What/How + oracle/raw arms). Exit: THE-030; incremental-build call-count test; first ledger rows with\ncodebase-QA receipts on two pinned repos.\nPhase 3 — memory\nScope: canonical facts (dual timestamps), two substrates, scoped trees with\ndirty-path refresh; retrieval lanes under the ported laws (union, RRF,\nconsensus-first, force-loads, sufficiency gate, concentration); temporal asOf reads; lifecycle ops; harness hooks (SessionStart…PreCompact) +\nmemory MCP tools; memory-bench (ability axes). Exit: THE-031…034; freshness (build-rate) and axis scores on\nLongMemEval-style + BEAM-style sets recorded in the ledger; PreCompact Σ\ncheckpoint demonstrably bounds loss.\nPhase 4 — kernel\nScope: coordinator + phase FSM + pools + verdicts; Σ + plan DAG + Π; guards; harness-claude; worktree/git discipline; roles (lead/planner/implementer/\nreviewer/verifier/merger/arbiter); tracks + gates (governance as policy\nvalues); kernel-sim suite on the fake harness; theia run, theia replay. Exit: THE-020…027, THE-054; sim stories for every guard and gate;\ntask-bench baseline (single-shot vs scaffold) with paired stats on a pinned\nset; first cost-accuracy frontier plot.\nPhase 5 — evaluation & governance hardening\nScope: the ledger as a doc + coverage check; two-tier acceptance harness;\nuncertainty calibration loop; diagnostician role (KRCA narrowing + skeleton\nprior + fingerprint reuse); anomaly events + incident path; vendor registry\nenforcement; audit anchoring. Exit: THE-040…044; a full pilot-shaped report (the competitor's KPI\ntriple + our determinism/freshness/cost metrics) produced end-to-end by theia eval.\nStatus — 2026-08-31, two implementation trains (feature-complete) Phase State\n0 — spine\nLANDED: terryid+HLC (proof-mirrored), event log (hash chain, replay fold), OCC generations, blobs, YAML config; 23 tests\n1 — gateway\nLANDED: seam vocabulary, masking, cost lock, record/replay, retry; codex (subscription, resume), fake, OpenAI embeddings, local ONNX embeddings (the default; gateway.embeddings policy + read-seam identity law); live smoke receipts (ledger §1)\n2 — codebase model\nLANDED: tree-sitter registry + 13-language AST facts + query tools; incremental intent index (live receipts); retrieval union over the shared indexes ( index reindex / search ); MCP exposure via theia serve\n3 — memory\nLANDED: dual-timestamp facts, version chains, near-dup gate, redacted chunks, generation indexes (tantivy+faiss), lane retrieval w/ consensus, as-of reads, scoped trees (dirty-path refresh), lifecycle maintain + GC, Claude Code hooks (context/recall/PreCompact checkpoints); live receipts. This train: local embeddings default, the Snowball lexical analyzer (receipt-promoted), the memory bench — retrieval and QA halves (notes/10 — the improve-loop instrument behind 18 §7), the bag- parity test, the sentence tier (built, opt-in until its receipt). Parked: asOf vector lane (ledger §3)\n4 — kernel\nLANDED: FSM-as-data, proved Σ, plan DAG+Π, coordinator+guards, gateway role runner + prompt overrides, command verifier, theia run live end-to-end; worktree isolation + real merger, policy-bounded delegation, fresh-perspective dispatch\n5 — eval/governance\nLANDED: ledger + replay equality + audit CLI; otel facade (Prometheus /metrics, OTLP→Jaeger tracing), anomaly taxonomy (AgentOps classes, drift-tested), diagnostician (rule table + fingerprint reuse), calibration (armed; awaiting logprob-bearing transport), index-bench. Deferred by the roadmap table: jail, workflow synthesis; parked with fallback: asOf vector lane\nSince that table, three further trains landed (every mechanism with its\nledger §1 receipt; decision history in git + the ledger): the review train\n(run views incl. the TUI default, copy workspaces + sandbox tiers with both\nfull-access cells probed live, plan artifacts, clarify/interactivity,\nresume/steer/fork/compare, stdin briefs, index status, structured commits,\nmulti-root workspaces with the changeset envelope, the asOf vector lane, theia jail ), the autonomy wave (verifier workspace scoping, the agent-side\nMCP belt + rules-SoT ladder, context alerts, the CLI-first Claude harness\nwith cross-family review receipts, task-targeted steering + probe/ask, the\nhome screen + @file briefs, dagger-grade TUI polish, freshness\nautomation, codex hooks install, closest-scope-first per-root overlays),\nand the eval train ( theia eval swebl — the SWE-bench-Live three-posture\nharness; the C# smoke ladder itself is parked mid-flight with full pickup\nstate in notes/06-eval-plan.md §7). Demo\nwalkthrough: demo.md.\nQueued (decided, not started — re-enters by the chief's call) Item State\nEVAL-SMOKE\nEXECUTED 2026-09-13 (receipt docs/eval/receipts/swebl-cs-smoke-r2.md ): full 3×3 matrix — median RESOLVED by S0.5 ($0.05) and S1 ($2.36) over S0's miss (the ladder's designed shape), easy closed unresolved-by-instance-ambiguity at N=5 with every mechanical axis fixed+verified, hard excluded-with-reason (gold stable 97/106 on this box). NEXT: N=3 on the resolved pair before any claimed delta, a wider cs draw (n≈10) for signal, then the theia-ts20 stage per notes/06\nDocument ingestion & understanding\nkind: DocSource — prose ingestion (local globs first — the.md-invisible gap — then MCP list/fetch sources), content-addressed cache, heading-aware chunks, docs:<source> generation lanes unioned into retrieval; PLUS document understanding: PDF/Excel/Word/image parsing + indexing, and the orchestrator's task analysis consuming such artifacts when a tracker MCP hands over an issue with attachments (attachment ingestion feeds plan/context collection, not only retrieval). Approach fixed: heuristic-first structure parsing per the Oracle visual-element paper (vector-line/alignment tables, form tiers, caption association, fragment merge, watermark filtering; NO VLM in the hot path — fits low-end boxes); charts via the OneChart model (franken_ocr as the runner reference); a claude-skill-style PDF extraction workflow REPLACES the default pdf parsing tool. Excluded with reason: Baidu Unlimited-OCR cannot run on low-end machines (the hardware bound goes in the ledger row when this lands). Full design now in 19-documents.md (element trees, fragment edges, KEY-summary descent, media sidecars; M12–M17)\nLead accept verdict\nSHIPPED 2026-09-07 (ledger §1): escalated --lead_accept--> merge, unverified-accept refused, vendored prompt law — earned by the 34521 r2 full-scale receipt (verified-done work discarded, dependents stranded)\nFork-owned run branches\na fork of a run-branch run resumes in-checkout today (the parent-branch aliasing hazard is refused, not silently taken — cmd-run says so); fork mode should cut its own branch from the parent branch tip\nMid-run agent-callable RCA\nrun_diagnose from inside a live run (today: post-hoc over stored logs)\nDaemon live-message delivery\ncross-process msg reaching a live run immediately (today: next-rehydration with the honest hint)\nFact-edge graph\nhop pre-computation at memory ingest (pseudo-query ANN links, density cap, tombstoned generations; source receipt 0.617→0.842 multi-hop) — earns its slot behind a multi-hop workload; docs/07 §3\nMemory depth\ntargeted deletion by provenance ref (GDPR-shaped); decay/merge consolidation; the retrieval stages carrying laws 7–9 (force-loads, sufficiency gate, block concentration) — each re-earns its receipt on our benchmarks. The scene/topic family is superseded by the world-view group layer, designed with the adaptive-retrieval + context-governance set in 18-memory-structure.md (M1–M11, phased receipts §7)\nask blueprint-slice lane\narchitecture-shaped questions under-retrieve on the k=6 union (the repomix-eval receipt, notes/07) — force-load top-importance blueprint entries for how-shaped asks\nTask-scoped blueprint projection\nthe intelligent successor to the complete-blueprint posture (docs/05 §5): touched entries + their R-closure + the importance-9 backbone, receipt-gated against the complete map on the eval ladder — a projection that loses to the full map is the AOCI partial-view defect class re-invented. Since 2026-09-07 the plan artifact carries the SAME map the run injects (caps removed; the approval digest therefore couples to the map's content — accepted until this redesign, where a map-generation digest may become the record) and run.blueprintMinImportance is the interim dose control at window-exceeding scale (notes/20). Analysis + design + evaluation = its own session (notes/18)\nPII granular control\nredesign of the data-class machinery for per-class, per-path control (which detector classes block vs mask vs pass, per calling surface); supersedes the interim indexer mask-not-block exception on THE-014 (docs/05 §2). Until it lands, the detector set stays small and high-precision, and the indexer masks (notes/18)\nVacuous-check warning at plan time\nSHIPPED 2026-09-12: the pre-flight names every task whose EVERY check passes at base (green→green attests nothing; the demo-cs run 2ak7y7cquk… merged a fix on exactly such a check) — a red first entry (red→green) stays silent; (root HEAD, command)-keyed pre-flight reuse still queued if plan-time latency ever warrants a cache\nVendor semantic loader\nselection-as-retrieval over the vendored rules/skills set (~200 docs): BM25+embedding index as a treeHash-keyed generation, Summary-line routing, per-role budget policy values, force-load pins, digest-audited selection — full design in notes/08-vendor-context-semantic-loader.md; unified with the workspace/user rules & skills layer in 20-rules-skills.md (one selection engine, funnel stages as measured escalation); NEXT SESSION's work\nVetted profiles\nkind: Profile — a curated bundle of MCP servers (auto-install specs, env-var NAMES only), belt tool grants, context entries, and key-wise policy overlays; one new outermost layer under the T-Z precedence, pinned by source+hash, offered at init/setup with a vendored generic default; company tier = per-org profile repos via fine-grained PATs (privacy-preserving distillation of learned memory into profile rules is deliberately later). Full design in notes/13-vendored-profiles.md; step 1's receipt is a null (resolved config byte-identical with and without the layer)\nReal-task Azure eval\ntwo completed C# tasks from a production Azure DevOps project (one easy-medium, one product-understanding-bound), reconstructed at lastMergeCommit^1 of their linked PRs, run under the swebl posture ladder with the environment skill + C#/MSSQL rules injected; graded by the reference PR's own tests + a review-shaped judge; the case-2 axis: with vs without the ticket-comments/wiki evidence set. Protocol in notes/14-azure-eval-protocol.md; blocked on a read-only PAT\nObservability follow-ups\nper-task diff view over kept audit branches; role-lane TUI grouping (the overseer agents-tab fold); live assistant text behind the spinner; harness session import by recorded sessionId; exchange-blob retention policy — queue + shapes in notes/12-observability-completeness.md §4\nRelease pipeline first live pass\ncut v0.2.N-pre.1 via cut-release.mts, watch release.yml build+publish+verify on hosted runners, promote via promote.yml (typed confirmation), confirm retention prune applies (the local --apply was permission-blocked this session; the dry-run receipt stands)\nSecond-reviewer promote gate\nenvironment: release-approval with required reviewers on the approve job — the API refuses the protection rule on the current plan (422, billing); re-add when the plan supports it\nMechanical release immutability\ntag ruleset on theia-releases ( {\"target\":\"tag\",\"rules\":[{\"type\":\"update\"},{\"type\":\"non_fast_forward\"}]} on refs/tags/v*, deletion allowed) + the immutable-releases setting — both 403 on the current plan; docs/17 §3 carries the by-construction posture until then\nDeferred (owned, scoped, not started) Item Scope sketch Blocked on\nSession continuity as Σ projection\nre-cut run.sessionContinuity to off | sigma-shared (notes/15): shared cross-round state = the Σ-projected stable prefix only; prompt stable-ordering for provider prefix-cache (land first, pure win); per-harness checkpoint-fork probes; the continuity receipt then gates the default. Follow-on: Self-GC controllable-accumulation increment (read sgc-/sskill- first, notes/15 §7) to ground review-loops without uncurated resume\nthis session's eval receipt + the paper analysis\nJail residuals\nthe bwrap theia jail + jailed-full tier are shipped (10-integrations §6); deferred residuals: full child-subreaper sweep, per-harness RW registry beyond codex, --unshare-net policy\ndemand\nFUSE mount\nread views over.theia/ (rendered cards, run timelines)\nPhase 3 layouts frozen\nPostgres backend\nstore-postgres passing the conformance suites; local stores become cooperative in-progress state; multi-writer node ids in terryid\na real multi-user deployment demand\nWorkflow synthesis\ncoop/nexus-style role-graph synthesis + IPR loop, gated experimental\nPhase 4 + eval receipts to judge it\nAdditional harnesses\nopencode/gemini adapters (translational only)\ndemand + fixtures\nExternal T-SQL parser (Rust/Go)\nupgrade the SQL facts hybrid's scan arm with a real T-SQL AST (sqruff / sqlparser-rs class, prebuilt binary, doctor-probed, honest degrade to the shipped hybrid — the jail's probe pattern)\ndemand: a corpus where the hybrid measurably under-extracts\nWindows as a first-class target\nRUNTIME LANDED (notes/23): git-bash spawn substrate, portable copies/kills/reaper, AppContainer jail backend, launcher windows keys, build-sea windows cell — receipts on the eval VM; remaining: hosted CI windows cell (gated on the local build receipt), install.ps1, PATH polish\neval receipts → release-cell follow-up\nContainer tool provider\ndocker-wrapped external agents/tools as graph nodes (coop shape), riding the jail's spawn-wrapper seam\ndemand\nStanding risks, named\nCodex/Claude surface drift — harness CLIs and hook surfaces change\nunder us. Mitigation: adapters are thin + fixture-tested; doctor probes\ndetect breakage at startup, not mid-run (THE-013).\nIndex/summary cost at scale — intent entries and tree summaries are\nmodel-priced. Mitigation: importance budgets, hash-gated regeneration,\nbuild-rate as a first-class benchmark (landed: build-rate observability + theia eval index-bench — receipt in ledger §1).\nDeterminism leaks — any un-evented nondeterminism breaks THE-043.\nMitigation: replay CI from Phase 0 so leaks surface the week they enter.\nDoc rot — this set is prose. Mitigation: the invariant catalog carries\nthe checkable core; receipts live in the ledger; prose defers to both.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/16-release.html":{"title":"Release & distribution — pipeline, channels, installer, vendor deliverables","content":"1. The failure this closes\nTool distribution fails users four ways: installers that guess wrong (an\namd64 binary on an arm Mac, a curl|sh that overwrites config), version\ndrift that cannot be pinned, releases that ship unverified (the artifact\nbuilt fine — nobody ran it the way a customer would), and updates that\ncannot be escaped when they break. The pipeline below closes all four:\nartifacts are verified AS A CUSTOMER before promotion (THE-060), versions\nare automatic and causally ordered, the installer doubles as the recovery\nlever (THE-063), and the vendor's prompt/rule deliverables update by hash\nout of band of binary releases (THE-065).\n2. The three repositories Repo Visibility Role\ndokimelabs/get-theia\npublic (GitHub Pages)\nthe first-stage installer: curl -fsSL https://dokimelabs.github.io/get-theia/install.sh | sh — detects os/arch, prompts for the PAT (or $THEIA_PAT ), resolves the latest RELEASE (or the $THEIA_VERSION pin), sha256-verifies, run-verifies BEFORE the symlink flips, seeds vendor deliverables, offers first-run setup. Also the documentation site: the landing/download page plus the Diplodoc-built user guide ( docs/ ) and platform reference ( reference/ ), built in CI by publish-artifact-repos.yml from docs/sources/; install.sh stays at the published root (THE-063)\ndokimelabs/theia-releases\nprivate\nrelease artifacts only; downloads require an end-user/company PAT — distribution IS the access gate\ndokimelabs/theia-dev\nprivate\nthe source + ALL workflows; RELEASES_PAT is the publish credential — a fine-grained token owned by dokimelabs, scoped to theia-releases only (Contents: read+write), verified to 404 on theia-dev\nThe org's base permission is read; the artifact repos carry no history\nand no collaboration, and the fork-based contribution flow with the /merge gate is 17-collaboration.md.\nWindows runtime support is landed (notes/23; the launcher accepts windows-x86_64/aarch64 keys and build-sea carries the cell) — the hosted\nrelease cell + install.ps1 follow the local build receipt. Intel macOS\n(darwin/x86_64) is out of the release set — refused BY NAME by launcher\nand installer.\n3. The pipeline — pre-release → verify → approve → release\noperator: cut-release.mts ──tag v0.2.N-pre.M──▶ release.yml build-matrix (3 cells + vendor) ─▶ publish PRE-RELEASE ─▶ verify (3 targets, customer-shaped: public installer + THEIA_VERSION pin + harness-fake smoke) │ green\noperator: promote.yml (manual dispatch + typed version confirmation) provenance check (verified run at the SAME commit; no double-promote) ─▶ rebuild at that commit, bare version baked ─▶ publish RELEASE ─▶ post-verify (same customer path ×3) ─▶ on failure: automatic retraction ─▶ prune retention (dry-run printed, then applied)\nOnly v*-pre.N tags build. A plain v* tag fails the guard loudly:\ncustomer releases exist only by promotion (THE-060/061).\n1a. No Actions artifacts, by construction. The pipeline stages\neverything on a DRAFT release: prepare creates it, build cells gh release upload straight into it, publish merges the\nper-platform checksums into one checksums.txt and flips\ndraft→published atomically — release-asset storage carries bytes the\nrelease needs anyway, so the free-org Actions quota has no surface\nleft. The quota blocked publishing TWICE before this design\n(v0.2.199: 3.2GB of stale 90-day-retention transport artifacts;\nv0.2.201 first attempt: a stale over-quota billing snapshot refusing\nuploads hours after every artifact was deleted — usage recalculates\nevery 6-12h, and deletion does not un-block within the lag). A failed\nrun leaves the draft — invisible to customers, reused by a re-run of\nthe same tag; promote's approve sweeps a stale draft and its rollback\nretracts draft or published alike.\nVerification is the customer's path, not the builder's: the runner\ncurls the PUBLIC installer, pins THEIA_VERSION to the pre-release, and\nruns scripts/verify-install.sh — version match, doctor, init, mem\nadd/search (the native-runtime receipt), and a full kernel run routed to harness-fake 's green rulebook ( THEIA_FAKE_RULEBOOK=green ): real FSM,\nreal verify-command execution, real event log — only the model\nsynthesized (THE-041). Exit 0 + \"audit chain: intact\" required.\nPromotion is a manual act — workflow_dispatch with the pre-tag AND\na typed repeat of the release version (the fat-finger gate). On a plan\nwith private-repo environment protection, add environment: release-approval to the approve job for a second\nreviewer. The rebuild differs from the verified bytes ONLY in the baked\nversion (pre-releases bake -pre.N so theia version is honest for\ndevelopers; releases bake the bare version so launcher arithmetic stays\nexact) — and post-verify re-runs the whole customer smoke on the\nreleased bytes, retracting the release automatically on failure.\n4. Versions — automatic patch, operator-owned tagging\nVERSION holds MAJOR.MINOR (a deliberate operator edit). The patch is\nthe commit count since VERSION last changed ( scripts/version.mts ), so\nevery commit owns a unique, growing, causally ordered version — the Chrome\nshape — with zero bookkeeping. scripts/cut-release.mts turns HEAD into\nthe next v<ver>-pre.N tag and pushes (refusing a dirty tree); WHEN to cut\nand WHEN to promote stay human decisions. Ordering (releases above their\nprereleases, numeric fields) is compareVersions in the launcher —\ntested, and used by staleness so a pinned newer build is never nagged\n\"update available\" toward an older latest. The considered alternative —\nrelease-please's conventional-commit automation (see ~/work/meridian) —\nwas rejected for coupling version arithmetic to commit-message discipline;\nthe git-count scheme needs none.\n5. Channels, the launcher, self-management\n~/.config/theia/config.yaml (0600):\ngithub: { pat: ghp_… } # release download, staleness, self update openai: { apiKey: sk-… } # openai embedding posture only (OPENAI_API_KEY env wins); local models: ~/.cache/theia/models dev: { root: ~/work/theia-dev } update: { channel: release } # 'prerelease' = developer opt-in (THE-062)\nEnd users resolve releases/latest, which by GitHub API contract never\nreturns a pre-release; the prerelease channel (set only by theia setup --channel prerelease, never the installer) resolves the\nnewest version INCLUDING -pre.N builds. Explicitly selecting a\npre-release always requires the exact suffix ( --x-release 0.2.17-pre.1 ) —\nartifact names carry it, so a typo'd bare version cannot match (THE-061).\nThe launcher resolution order ( --dev > --x-release > THEIA_X_RELEASE\nself), the cache under ~/.cache/theia/releases/<v>, checksum\nverification, and the loud no-silent-fallback error (THE-017) are\nunchanged. theia self update updates the binary per channel AND runs the\nvendor phase (§9) on both arms.\n5a. First-run setup\nBare theia on a terminal with an unconfigured environment (no user\nconfig and/or no workspace upward) offers the guided setup instead of\nhelp: global config (PAT + OpenAI key, hidden input) → project init at cwd\n(optional multi-root; the home project is the primary root) → harness\nchoice seeded by the doctor probes → credential guidance naming each\nfamily's login command. Configured environments keep the home screen;\nnon-TTY keeps help. theia setup --interactive is the global half alone —\nthe installer offers it after a fresh install when the config lacks keys.\n6. Issuing end-user tokens\nDistribution IS the access gate, so \"licensing a customer\" = minting a\ntoken that can download releases and nothing else. GitHub has no API for\ncreating PATs, so scripts/mint-release-token.mts <customer> replicates\nthe device-auth SHAPE around the web mint: it opens the fine-grained token\npage pre-filled via GitHub's template-URL parameters (name, description,\ntarget_name, expires_in — MINT_EXPIRES_DAYS overrides the 90-day\ndefault — and contents=read; repository SELECTION has no URL parameter\nand is the one manual click, called out in the printout), and verifies the pasted\ntoken before delivery — installer-shaped reads must pass, and ANY\nvisibility into theia-dev refuses the token as over-broad (the\noperator's own broad token fails this gate; receipt in the commit log).\nIt prints the sha256 fingerprint (the audit handle; the value is never\nstored) and the customer install snippet.\n--device runs the REAL RFC 8628 device grant (the gh auth login mechanism) for the collaborator alternative: the customer's GitHub account\nis invited read-only to theia-releases, THEIA_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID names a\nonce-registered OAuth app, the script prints the code + opens the\nverification page + polls to authorization — an operator-assisted session\nmint. The resulting token passes the SAME verification gates, so a\ncustomer who can see theia-dev is refused identically. One token per\ncustomer; revocation = delete that token. The many-customers upgrade (a\nGitHub App vending short-lived installation tokens) stays roadmap-shaped.\n7. Retention — prune by policy, pin by contract\nscripts/prune-releases.mts keeps: the 2 newest releases, the single\nnewest pre-release, every version pinned in release-pins.yaml (version +\ncommit + reason + customer — the support contract), and drafts; everything\nelse is deleted from theia-releases, release AND tag. Dry-run by default,\ndestructive only under --apply; the promote workflow prints the dry-run\nbefore applying (THE-064). When a release is pruned, its theia-dev tag\nis deleted by the operator in the same pass — an old tag re-pushed later\nexecutes the release workflow of ITS OWN tree, which may predate the\npromotion guard (ledger §4). Ordering is compareVersions; a mis-flagged\ntag with a -pre. suffix counts as a prerelease either way.\n8. Artifacts & the verification scenario\ntheia-v<ver>-<os>-<arch>.tar.gz (SEA executable + lib/ native runtime\n— sqlite, faiss, tantivy, tree-sitter grammars, and onnxruntime for local\nembeddings, installed with ONNXRUNTIME_NODE_INSTALL=skip so the 1 GB\nCUDA providers never enter a customer artifact — + lib/vendor/ deliverables), checksums.txt (per-platform files\nconcatenated at publish — the merge-multiple pothole, ledger §4), vendor-manifest.json, and theia-vendor-<treeHash>.tar.gz. The\nfunctional gate is scripts/verify-install.sh (§3 item 2) — runnable\nlocally against any installed binary: EXPECT_VERSION=… THEIA_BIN=… sh scripts/verify-install.sh.\n9. Vendor deliverables — prompts/rules/skills by hash\ndistribution/vendor/ in theia-dev is the tree WE ship (docs and\nREADME there): prompts/kernel.yaml is the ONLY role-prompt source — no compiled copy\nexists in code; a dev tree reads this same file lazily, and rules/ + skills/ carry the architectural rules and technology playbooks (the\ngrowing ~200-document set the semantic-loader milestone selects over;\ndocs/13). Runtime resolution: THEIA_VENDOR_DIR > ~/.local/share/theia/vendor/current (auto-updated) > lib/vendor beside\nthe executable (embedded) > the repo's distribution/vendor dev tree. A broken or (in an embedded\nbuild) unresolvable tree FAILS the kernel verbs loudly naming the repair;\nversion/setup/self/doctor/init stay runnable — they are the way out\n(THE-065).\nPackaging: scripts/build-vendor.mts names the artifact by the vendor\ntree's GIT TREE HASH (identical content = identical identity across\ncommits; a dirty tree refuses to package) with a per-file sha256 manifest.\nEvery release carries the manifest + tarball; vendor.yml additionally\nships vendor-only updates to BOTH channels' newest published versions on\nany distribution/vendor/ change — the same release version can carry newer vendor\ncontent. Auto-update ( theia self update, both arms) consults the release OF THE\nVERSION THE MACHINE RUNS — never blindly the newest: fetch that release's\nSMALL manifest, compare treeHash — equal means done with no artifact\ndownload (the checksums-only fast path) — else download the tarball,\nverify every file's sha256, install under the hash, flip current atomically. Every release carries its own manifest INSIDE the artifact\ntoo, so a pruned release still verifies and reports correspondence fully\noffline; theia doctor prints exactly that: the resolved set verified\nagainst its manifest, and whether it matches this release's embedded\nhashes or is a newer auto-updated set. The installer seeds current from\nthe embedded copy when absent.\nPrompt-consumption today: role prompts load from the resolved vendor tree\nat every invocation (user.theia/prompts overrides still append after —\nclosest scope last); rules/skills reach agent context via explicit\nContext-manifest references. The semantic selection layer over the full\nvendored set is designed in notes/08 and scheduled in docs/13.\n10. CI\nci.yml is path-scoped (dorny/paths-filter): TypeScript and Coq jobs run\nonly when their inputs moved. build-matrix.yml is the reusable build\n(3 SEA cells + the vendor artifact; version injected — release builds\nnever re-derive it on a detached checkout). release.yml, promote.yml, vendor.yml per §3/§9. The SEA build script speaks zx (A20) and\nauto-fetches an official Node base when the running Node lacks the SEA\nfuse (the Fedora-shim pothole, ledger §4).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/17-collaboration.html":{"title":"Collaboration — forks, the merge gate, artifact repositories","content":"1. The model\nNobody pushes to dokimelabs/theia-dev but the pipeline and the operator.\nThe org's base permission is read (verified live); developers are org\nmembers or repository collaborators with read — enough to see, fork, and\nopen PRs, never to write. The flow:\nDeveloper forks, clones THEIR fork, commits, pushes the branch to the\nfork, opens a PR against main.\nCI runs on the PR as usual.\nThe operator reviews and types /merge as a PR comment.\nThe fork-merge bot (.github/workflows/fork-merge.yml ) enforces, in\norder: the commenter holds admin/maintain (the operator's act); the PR\nauthor is an org member or collaborator (membership is the license to\ncontribute — read-only suffices); every PR commit passes the email-privacy gate (§4); and the merge lands EXACTLY the head\nsha visible when /merge was typed ( --match-head-commit — a push\nracing the approval fails loudly instead of smuggling commits).\nThe landed commit is a squash authored by the contributor (their\nname/email; GitHub attributes squash commits to the PR author) with the\nbot as committer — attribution stays honest, write access stays zero.\nThe bot composes the squash message itself (PR title + body) and\napplies the attribution law (§5) to its trailers.\n2. Artifact repositories — no history, no collaboration\nget-theia (public) and the theia-releases README are generated\nartifacts: their source lives in distribution/{get-theia,theia-releases}/ here (beside the vendored deliverables tree, distribution/vendor/ —\ndocs/16 §9), and publish-artifact-repos.yml (plus scripts/publish-artifact-repo.mts )\nforce-pushes each as a SINGLE orphan commit on every change (via the PUBLISH_TOKEN secret — it must reach both artifact repos; the\nfine-grained RELEASES_PAT deliberately cannot, ledger §4) — the repos\ncarry one commit at all times (verified live: commit count 1 in both). get-theia has issues/wiki/projects disabled and auto-closes PRs (a\npublic repo cannot disable them) with the pointer to theia-dev. Release\ntags in theia-releases are untouched by the branch replace — tags pin\ntheir own objects (all releases verified intact post-publish).\n3. Release immutability on this plan\nPublished releases are immutable-but-deletable by construction today:\nonly the pipeline holds write (org base read; the classic operator token\nlives solely in CI secrets), the publish/promote workflows never edit a\npublished release (the vendor manifest attach is the single sanctioned\naddition), retention DELETES releases but never rewrites them, and every\ncustomer download is sha256-verified against checksums.txt — a mutated\nartifact fails closed at install. Mechanical enforcement (a tag ruleset\nblocking update / non_fast_forward on refs/tags/v* while allowing\ndeletion, and the immutable-releases repo setting) is refused on the\ncurrent plan (403, \"Upgrade to GitHub Pro or make this repository\npublic\") — the exact calls are in the roadmap row and apply the day the\nplan supports them.\n4. Email privacy — noreply addresses only\nThe policy: no commit in any dokimelabs repository carries a personal\nemail — author and committer are <id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com (or GitHub's own noreply@github.com web-flow identity). What GitHub can\nand cannot enforce on this plan, probed live:\nServer-side rejection of non-noreply pushes is not available. GitHub's \"block command line pushes that expose my email\" toggle is a\nper-USER setting (each member enables it for their own account; the org\ncannot mandate it). Org-level rulesets answer 403 on the free plan\n(\"Upgrade to GitHub Team\" — verified), and the rule that would carry the\npolicy ( commit_author_email_pattern, a metadata restriction) requires\nGitHub Enterprise even above Team. The upgrade-day call, ready to run: POST /orgs/dokimelabs/rulesets with target: branch, all repos/refs,\nand a commit_author_email_pattern rule\n( operator: ends_with, pattern: \"@users.noreply.github.com\" ).\nEnforcement therefore lives at the ingress points, which this\nrepository controls completely: the operator's pushes use the repo-local\npinned noreply identity (uniform across all history — re-verified after\nthe message rewrite); the ONLY other write path is the fork-merge bot,\nwhose email gate refuses /merge while any PR commit carries a\nnon-noreply author or committer email, with the fix named in the\nrefusal (enable \"keep my email addresses private\", rewrite the branch,\nforce-push the fork).\nDetection backs prevention: ci.yml's email-policy job fails the\nbuild if a non-noreply email ever lands on main — the drift alarm for\nmisconfigured operator clones.\n5. Attribution — models assist, they do not co-author\nSquash messages composed by the fork-merge bot rewrite any Co-Authored-By: trailer naming a model assistant (Claude, Codex,\nCopilot, Gemini, Cursor, Devin, Aider, …) into the Assisted-By: form, one line per distinct assistant — aligned with the emerging\ndisclosure conventions that reserve authorship for accountable persons\nwhile keeping the assistance disclosed. Human co-author trailers pass\nthrough untouched; Claude-Session: links are dropped (they point at\nprivate sessions and are meaningless to readers of a public history).\nThe repo's own history follows the same law: the message rewrite that\nnormalized it removed model co-author trailers wholesale, and new\noperator commits carry none.\n6. Issue-ops — comment verbs on issues\nThe /merge model extended to issues: members hold read on code (the §1\npolicy) plus the triage repository role (issue management, zero code\nwrite), and the audited verbs below ride.github/workflows/issue-ops.yml ( issue_comment -triggered, /merge-gated: org membership checked, refusals\nname the missing right, acks are 👍 reactions). The comment trail is the\nsystem of record for commitments and hours.\nVerb Who Effect\n/assign [@user]\nany org member\nself-claim (the commitment moment) or assign any member\n/unassign [@user]\nany org member\nremove assignment\n/label <l> [<l>…]\nany org member\nlabels from the seeded taxonomy ONLY — unknown names are refused (the vocabulary cannot drift by typo); clears needs-triage\n/estimate <N>h\nany org member\nsets the board's Estimate (h) and snaps the size/* label UP into the enclosing bucket (3h→S, 5h→M, 9h→L; XL demands a split)\n/spent <N>h\nassignee or maintainer\nACCUMULATES into Spent (h); the ⏱ reply line is the audit and recovery source\n/due YYYY-MM-DD\nany org member\nsets the board's Due (required on every commitment -labeled issue)\n/close · /reopen\nassignee or maintainer\nstate moves\n/commands\nanyone\nreplies with this table\nMechanics: issue mutations ride the workflow's own github.token ( issues: write, declared per job); board-field writes ride the PROJECT_TOKEN secret (fine-grained: org projects read+write plus\ntheia-dev issues read, nothing else — gate-verified before install, the\n§16-token discipline applied to people-facing automation). With the\nsecret absent the verbs still work and the ⏱ / 📅 reply lines carry the\ndata — the field lags, honestly. /help is deliberately unclaimed: the\nProw dialect (the most widely known comment-ops vocabulary) uses it to\napply the help-wanted label, so claiming it for documentation would\nmislead exactly the contributors most familiar with the pattern.\nOne field is a deliberate mirror: the board's single-select Area duplicates the area/* label ONLY because table views cannot group by\nthe multi-value Labels field (and views have no API to script around\nit). The label stays the source of truth; the mirror is machine-owned —\nsynced on every labeled / unlabeled event and re-swept weekly by the\ndigest job — so it can never drift by hand.\nA weekly digest workflow posts velocity (Σ Estimate of non-retro issues\nclosed in the window), hours logged (the window's ⏱ lines), the open\ncommitment register with overdue flags, and the per-area costbook to the\npinned digest issue — velocity and engineering-hours as standing numbers,\nnot a report someone remembers to write.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/05-codebase-model.html":{"title":"Codebase model — intent index, AST facts, embeddings","content":"1. The failure this closes\nAn agent exploring a repository at runtime rebuilds a different mental model\nevery run, spends 4–130× the tokens of a precomputed map, and still ships a\ncharacteristic defect family: it modifies 2 of 4 query paths that read a\nfield, forgets the dependent view, guesses an interface contract, invents a\ncolumn (AOCI's 39 defects across 19 tasks vs 0 with an index; every cluster\nreproduced across stacks). Lexical search compounds it — grep Search cannot\ntell the class from the free function from the comment (InfCode's motivating\nambiguity), and the miss rate grows with the languages in play. The codebase model is Aletheia's precomputed, incrementally fresh,\nhash-verified answer: three tiers, one per failure mode.\n2. The three tiers Tier Content Producer Determinism\nIntent index\none entry per code unit (file / DB table / config): symbolic tag + semantic elements\nLLM via gateway\nstable across runs (same inputs ⇒ same entry, replayable)\nAST facts\nsymbols, definitions, references, inheritance/call/containment edges, signatures\ntree-sitter, per language\nfully deterministic, no LLM\nEmbedding lane\nvectors over intent entries + code chunks\nembedding transport\ndeterministic per (model, dims, text)\nIntent entry shape (AOCI, adopted with its ablation as the budget law): path[TAG]: F:role | R:related-paths | A:api | S:synopsis, where the tag\npacks architectural layer, business module, importance (the 9/8/7/5/3/1\nscale), tech markers, and size; importance scales the token budget (core\nfiles 80–150 tokens, peripheral 20–40). The S element gets the budget —\ndesign decisions, invariants, fallback logic, encryption/transaction notes —\nbecause removing it cost −20.1pp overall and −33pp on cross-file \"how\"\nquestions, the largest single-component effect AOCI measured; R (explicit\nrelations) is what dependency enumeration rides on (−20pp on \"what\" without\nit). Entries are mutually independent — R holds path references, never\nembedded content — which is what makes maintenance O(1) per changed file.\nDatabase tables get their own entries (domain/type/scale/features + field\nnotes): schema-blindness was one of the four measured defect clusters.\nEntry acceptance is guidance-plus-nets. The indexer prompt carries the\nlength guidance (word budgets by importance, \"aim under 1200 chars for S\")\nand the schema enforces hard caps at ~5× that guidance — the first\ncustomer index dropped 25 of 987 CORRECT replies at a strict seam (a\n727-line aggregate root died 262 chars over a cap inside regular answer\nvariance; each hole later cost ~$5 of a session hand-reading the\nunindexed file against $0.10 at build). Three acceptance mechanisms, all\nobservable and none silent: (1) unknown reply keys are stripped, never\na rejection — the seven known fields still validate strictly, so nothing\nunvalidated passes; (2) off-scale importance snaps DOWN to the nearest\nlegal value; (3) every reply crosses the JSON healer ( @theia/json-repair — context-aware tolerant parsing for fences,\nunescaped inner quotes, truncation) before the schema, with healed writes\ncounted on the build report, the index.build audit event, and a\ntelemetry counter. The nets stay real: a runaway blob still refuses.\nThe index is complete by contract. An indexer call whose file trips a\nprohibited-content detector is masked, not blocked — the gateway\nreplaces the span with its placeholder and proceeds, counts visible on the\naudit event (the one named exception to THE-014, marked CONCEPT-CHANGE:\na blocked intent call leaves a permanent map hole while the secret stays\non this machine either way). Granular per-class control arrives with the\nPII redesign ( 13-roadmap.md ).\nAST facts close the intent tier's measured weakness — verbatim identifier\nenumeration is lossy under semantic compression — with deterministic query\ntools exposed to agents and MCP hosts: find_symbol, list_references, inheritance_chain, call_graph(fn), signature(fn) (InfCode's toolset,\ngeneralized across tree-sitter grammars — shipped languages: TypeScript/TSX/JavaScript, Python, Go, Java, Kotlin, C, C++, C#, Objective-C, Swift,\nBash-family shell, and SQL). SQL is the hybrid arm: real T-SQL defeats\nits grammar exactly at the definition headers ( CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE [dbo].[X] @p … errors out; an SSMS schema dump parsed 0 of 418 tables),\nso tables/views/procedures/functions and their referenced tables come from\na deterministic comment- and string-stripped header scan beside the tree\nwalk — schema scripts, migrations, and stored procedures land in the\nintent index and the symbol lane like any code file (schema blindness is\none of the four measured defect clusters). A richer external T-SQL parser\n(sqruff / sqlparser-rs class) is deferred, optional\n( 13-roadmap.md ). InfCode's ablations carry the\nreceipt: −8.5pp resolution without AST search, −6.2pp without intent\nretrieval, and +17 average agent turns without both — the tools pay in\ntokens even before accuracy.\n3. Freshness — the incremental contract (A5)\nEach entry records (content_hash, builder_version, prompt_hash); theia index diffs hashes and regenerates exactly the changed entries —\nnever a whole-repo rebuild on the hot path.\nEntry batches publish as a generation behind the head\n( 03-storage.md §4 ); a crashed build is invisible.\nA level-triggered reconciler sweeps drift (renames, deleted files, builder\nupgrades) and repairs by diff — the \"index–code divergence erodes\neverything\" failure AOCI's platform section names is closed structurally. theia serve runs this loop: a periodic zero-model freshness check, budget-capped\nrebuild under indexPolicy: auto, and a staleness field on search\nreplies so host models KNOW when the map lags.\nGit integration: post-commit/worktree hooks mark dirty paths\n( theia hooks install git; the marks are a hint the differ reports —\nthe hash-diff stays the truth); the CI mode fails when the committed index head lags the tree\n(freshness as a measurable property — A19).\n4. Retrieval over the model\nQueries union the tiers, never trust one: intent-entry recall (lexical +\nvector) locates the subsystem; AST facts resolve the symbols precisely;\nchunk-level embeddings serve \"how is X implemented\" verbatim needs. The union\nrerank doctrine, budgets, and consensus rules follow the memory engine's\nmeasured laws ( 07-memory.md §5 ) — the codebase model is a\ncorpus like any other to the retrieval layer, with one addition: structure\nis routing, never evidence — a summary hit fans out to its members, and\nonly leaf content enters answers (the SKWH descent law).\nTwo presentation laws govern what leaves the retrieval surfaces: hits\ncarry the full entry (F, R, A, S plus the layer/module/importance\ncoordinates, on the MCP tool and the CLI verb alike — R is the measured\ndependency-enumeration carrier, and a score-plus-role line sends the\nreader straight back to grep), and the map travels with its decode\nkey — every surface that hands blueprint lines to a model prepends BLUEPRINT_LEGEND (an AOCI index is \"encoding rules followed by entries\";\nencoded lines without the rules ask the reader to guess the vocabulary).\n5. Consumers\nKernel roles receive the COMPLETE blueprint (legend + every entry,\nown root first in multi-root runs) in the stable span of every dispatch\nprompt — provider prefix caching pays for it after round one\n( 04-model-gateway.md §7 ), and the injected-\ncontext alert stays the loud guard on pathological sizes. Plan mode\ngets the same dose (run/plan parity, 2026-09-07): the planner's\nblueprint block and its kind: Context entries load whole into the\ncontent-hashed spec — the old 3,900/4,000-char cuts fed the planner ~1%\nof the map the roles execute against, and a truncated spec lied about\nwhat the approval digest attested. Where a complete multi-root map\nexceeds the model window (a 13-root production workspace rendered 3.8M\nchars ≈ 950K tokens — physically undispatchable), the operator sets run.blueprintMinImportance: entries below the floor leave the\ninjected map with a loud count line — the AOCI importance backbone,\nevery root still represented — while the belt tools reach every entry\non demand. AST tools ride the MCP belt on demand. A task-scoped\nprojection (touched entries + R-closure + the importance backbone) is\nthe queued intelligent successor ( 13-roadmap.md ) —\nit must beat the complete map on a receipt, not on intuition, because\nAOCI's defect clusters all trace to partial views.\nClaude Code / Codex sessions get the map through MCP tools and hooks\n( 10-integrations.md ) — the AOCI \"global context\nlayer beneath an agent pipeline\" hybrid, which their paper names as the\nnatural next step and never built.\nThe memory engine links facts to code units by entry id, so \"why does\nthis module exist\" answers survive refactors (rename ⇒ entry id stable,\ncontent hash changes).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/09-observability.html":{"title":"Observability — traces, metrics, cost, the audit chain","content":"1. The failure this closes\nAgent systems are harder to observe than microservices for a structural\nreason the AgentOps survey states plainly: in a service trace the payloads\nare stable API calls, but in an agent trace the inputs and outputs of every\nspan are model-generated — the uncertainty IS the signal — so tracing that\ndrops payloads drops the diagnosis, while tracing that keeps them leaks\nprompts and PII into monitoring. Meanwhile the audit requirement (competitor\n§9.0: every judgment fixed with agent_run_id, logs unwritable by the agent,\ntamper-evident, 3-year retention) is usually met with process promises.\nAletheia builds both — diagnosis-grade telemetry and mechanical\ntamper-evidence — into the seams, so features get them by construction (P2).\n2. Traces\nOTel with Jaeger export (OTLP, loaded only when OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT is set). Spans mirror the design objects —\nkernel rounds, per-job kernel.job spans (role, taskId, phase, attempt),\nand gateway calls — carrying structured attributes\n(role, mode, usage, cost) and payload references — digests into the\nevent log — never payload bodies. The full I/O is one command away for an\noperator with store access ( show --tui, the v viewer), and absent\nfrom the telemetry backend entirely. Finer nesting (per tool call)\nextends the same facade as the trace consumers appear.\nThe AgentOps checkpoint insight is native here: because every span's inputs\nare events, \"roll back to before step k and re-run\" is replay with a\ntruncated log — no separate checkpoint machinery.\nThe live display channel (the Dagger-bar live-visibility item,\nnotes/12 §5): adapters stream tool completions AS they happen —\nincremental JSONL feeders on the codex/claude stdout — through the\ngateway's onLiveTrace to the operator's live views: the TUI renders a\ndim in-flight activity line per dispatch (latest tool + running count,\nclosed by the audited call), --plain prints a live feed line per\ntool. Deliberately a DISPLAY channel, not an event path: the canonical tool.result events land at audit unchanged, so the log and replay\nequality are byte-identical with or without a viewer — and a call that\ndies mid-stream showed the operator its tools without ever recording\nevents a replay could not reproduce (the rejected incremental-log\nvariant's failure mode). Content-free by contract: argsShort only,\nbodies stay on the T-S blob path. The channel also carries call-start\npings and RETRY pings ( onRetry: attempt, wait, classified reason) — a\n429/500/session-limit backoff renders as ↻ retry 2/4 in 4s — … instead\nof a bare ticking clock (the spqr stuck-session report).\n3. Metrics\nPrometheus /metrics on the daemon; per-seam families mirroring the design\nobject (payment-platform's transitions metric, generalized): one counter per\nFSM transition, per verdict kind, per gateway result kind, per gate outcome,\nper reconciler diff; histograms for phase latency, gateway latency, block\nsizes; gauges for head lag (freshness — a first-class KPI, A19), queue\ndepths, and budget consumption. Cost metrics carry the locked-USD figures\n( 04-model-gateway.md §2 ); cost readouts ( theia show,\nthe home screen's run rows, eval reports) recompute nothing — they read\nwhat execution locked.\n4. The audit chain\nEvery event carries prev_digest — the log is a hash chain; any\nafter-the-fact edit breaks verification ( theia audit verify ). This is\nthe mechanical form of the competitor's WORM/tamper-evident requirement,\nwith periodic chain-head anchoring (a signed head digest to external\nstorage) as the optional hardening step.\nGateway events record model id, prompt digest, params digest, usage, and\nresult kind for every call — the agent_run_id + prompt_version + input hashes contract, complete by construction because no call bypasses the\nseam. Beside the digests, every audited call writes its exchange\nblob — the full system/prompt/reply text, class-gated (prohibited\nnever lands, restricted lands masked with visible counts) into the\nLOCAL content-addressed store, ref-only on the event (THE-055 holds\neverywhere but.theia/objects ) — so \"which prompt\" (the digest) and\n\"show me the prompt\" (TUI v on the gateway row) are both one step.\nThe harness session id rides the same event: the pointer into the\nharness's own transcript store for hook-connected sessions\n( notes/12 ).\nRetention is a policy value; GC respects it before physical deletion\n( 03-storage.md §6 ).\n5. PII discipline\nThe denylist is enforced at the boundary helpers in packages/otel (keys:\nprompt/content/email/token/secret classes), not at call sites — a caller\ncannot accidentally forward content into metrics or error tracking. Error\nmessages are sanitized against a closed allowlist of known runtime phrasings\nbefore they ride exceptions (model output never enters a log line — the\nFidant #3644 lesson: meta-key filtering does not see message strings).\nRedaction and masking counts are themselves metrics, so silence is\ndistinguishable from absence.\n6. Anomaly detection & self-diagnosis hooks\nThe AgentOps taxonomy (intra: reasoning/planning/action/memory/environment;\ninter: task-spec/security/communication/trust/emergent/termination) is the\nclosed label set for anomaly events the kernel and gateway emit (loop\ndetected, budget breach, verdict flapping, delegation depth, stale head,\nrefusal). These labels are the skeleton prior the diagnostician starts from\n( 06-orchestration.md §4 ) — detection feeds RCA\nfeeds resolution feeds experiential memory, the full AgentOps loop inside one\nplatform.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/08-evaluation.html":{"title":"Evaluation — determinism, simulation, benchmarks, the ledger","content":"1. The failure this closes\nAgentic systems ship claims that cannot survive contact: aggregate gains that\nconfound token budgets with mechanisms (the multi-agent literature's standing\ncritique — ledger §1), evaluators that lie (the LiveCodeBench harness's\nstateless stdin mock silently failed models by coding style — found only by\nreading transcripts), judges that flake inside contractual gates, and runs\nthat cannot be reproduced because the model, the clock, and the router all\nmoved. Aletheia's evaluation subsystem is built on the opposite bets:\nmechanical gates, replayable runs, pinned backends, receipts per mechanism,\nand honest negatives published with the same confidence as wins (R4).\n2. Determinism and replay (A22)\nEvery nondeterministic input is an event ( 01-architecture.md §4 ):\nmodel replies (by blob digest), tool outputs, clock reads, random draws.\nReplay equality is a tested property: theia replay <run> re-executes\nwith the gateway in replay mode (live calls refused) and must reproduce\nthe event stream byte-for-byte; divergence is a defect with a named owner\n(a nondeterminism leak). This is ReDel's replayable-log idea promoted from\ndebugging aid to invariant. The hybrid gateway mode (forks: replay\non recording hit, live-and-record on miss) deliberately does NOT weaken\nthis: strict replay remains the determinism gate and still throws on\nany miss; hybrid exists only so a fork's shared prefix re-serves free.\nOrdering is deterministic under concurrency: terryid tie-breaks\n( 02-identity.md §3 ); temporal query results have a\nunique coalesced encoding ( 03-storage.md §5 ).\n3. Simulation\nThe fake harness ( harness-fake ) swaps real model calls for (a) recorded\ngolden wire fixtures — byte-asserted per operation, psp-style — or (b)\nsynthesized weighted verdicts, overseer's --sim: stress the kernel's state\nmachine through thousands of runs at zero token cost, including the\nwind-down and stop paths ( --sim=N caps tickets so termination is\nexercised). The simulator is a first-class deployment mode, not a test\ndouble: benchmark and production run the SAME engine code with only the\ngateway backend swapped — the parity law that made the Fidant benchmark\nadapter trustworthy (their W28), adopted as an invariant.\n4. The ledger discipline\nEvery mechanism — a retrieval lane, a role, a threshold, a scaffold depth —\ngets one row in docs/ledger.md (created with the first measured mechanism)\nunder a verdict heading: KEPT-ON / KEPT-OPT-IN / OFF-CONFIRMED-NEGATIVE /\nABANDONED-REMOVED / PARKED, each with its receipt (benchmark delta +\nprovenance). The Fidant precedent is the reason: an engine with dozens of\ngates is unauditable from code alone — a gate reading off looks identical\nwhether unfinished or measured-harmful. Corollaries:\nA mechanism without a receipt does not default on.\nA measured negative is deleted (the code) and recorded (the row) — the\ncorpse marker prevents re-exploration (P11).\nThe config of record IS the coded default; a bare deployment runs the\nmeasured stack with zero env.\n5. Benchmarks Suite Status Measures Source shape\nkernel-sim\nshipped (the kernel test suite)\nFSM correctness, guard coverage, replay equality, crash-resume\nsynthesized verdicts, property-style\nindex-bench\nshipped ( theia eval index-bench )\ncold/incremental build rate, hash-gate call counts, locked cost\nsynthesized corpus, scripted gateway\ntask-bench\nshipped ( theia eval swebl )\nend-to-end resolution, cost, defects on pinned real instances\nSWE-bench-Live, the three-posture ladder, pinned backends ×N passes, paired per-instance stats (sign-flip permutation, McNemar on discordants) — protocol + receipts in notes/06-eval-plan.md\ncodebase QA\nplanned\nWhere/What/How accuracy of the codebase model vs oracle & raw-context arms\nAOCI's three-category design, ground truth by server-side grep; the repomix-eval probe ( notes/07 ) is its manual precursor\nmemory-bench\nshipped ( theia eval memory fetch/draw/retrieval/qa/report; notes/10 §4)\nretrieval per axis (single, multi-hop, temporal, knowledge-update, open-domain, verbatim, as-of): recall/coverage@k at turn and session grain, nDCG, MRR, leak (forbidden evidence); the QA half: end-to-end accuracy per axis incl. abstention (codex answerer over the retrieved block, LongMemEval's per-type judges, evidence-in-context reported beside accuracy), on a stratified subset of the same draw; paired per-question deltas between lane×analyzer×tier arms\nLoCoMo + LongMemEval (oracle, S) + a deterministic synthetic suite (planted verbatim facts, as-of cuts) through the PRODUCTION write path and retrieve; committed seeded draws under docs/eval/; small set ≤ 5 min cold on the 5 GB box, cached reruns free — axes diagnose; one aggregate hides\nfreshness/cost\nshipped as receipts (build-rate lines, locked USD, staleness metrics)\nindex lag, build rate, $/solved unit\nMemForest's input-normalized build rate; cost-vs-accuracy frontier plots ride the swebl report\nProtocol laws, learned the hard way by the corpus: pinned backends (gateway\nrouting noise dominated their gateway-served arms), refusals scored as\nfailures (never silently substitute a model), cap-matching made explicit when\narms differ, evaluator code itself under test (golden positive + golden\nnegative per checker), and per-problem agreement tables beside aggregates.\n6. Two-tier acceptance\nContractual gates are mechanical — answer_contains, exit codes, SQL\ncounts, replay equality — because a gate must not flake on a judge\n(eval-acceptance-golden). Quality reporting is judge-scored — the 0–1\ncomposite over correctness / must-contain / must-not-contain / hallucination,\nrolled into ability axes — with a human-double-marked calibration sample\npinning judge agreement. Golden sets follow the LongMemEval authoring method:\nanswers decompose into evidence statements distributed across positions, and\nthe case mix includes unanswerables and non-standalone follow-ups (MT-RAG's\nfinding: a set without them certifies a system that fails its first week).","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/12-invariants.html":{"title":"Invariants — the numbered catalog","content":"The enforceable law of the platform (claudexor's Bible shape, A3). Every\ninvariant has a stable id, never renumbered or reused; retirement leaves a RETIRED marker pointing at the successor. Each carries a verify: hint —\nthe test, gate, or review question that proves it; an invariant nobody can\ncheck is a wish. This file wins conflicts with every other doc and with\ncode; changing it is a deliberate, marked act (commit message carries CONCEPT-CHANGE(THE-NNN…) ), and a growing subset is pinned by executable\ncanary stories in the eval suites.\nFoundations\nTHE-001 One event-sourced SoT per concern; every other surface —\nindexes, Σ, summaries, dashboards — is derived, generation-stamped, and\nrebuildable by replay. verify: replay-equality suite; review question \"is\nthis new state derived or a second truth?\"\nTHE-002 Derived state publishes only via generation build-aside + OCC\nhead swap; failures leave garbage, never corruption; consumers accept only\nmonotonically newer generations. verify: concurrent-build test (exactly one\nwinner, no mixed reads); GC orphan test.\nTHE-003 Every id is a terryid: closed type-prefix registry, HLC-ordered\nbody; e hb f ⇒ id(e) < id(f) as strings. Stores persist the 22-char body;\nthe kind travels in the type/context; bodies are order-comparable across\nkinds. verify: proofs/hlc.v compiled in CI; property test over the encoder;\ncross-kind ordering test in terryid.\nTHE-004 Temporal queries are snapshot-reducible with unique coalesced\nencodings; the AG/BD bug fixtures pass. verify: golden temporal suite\ninstantiating snap_t(Q(R)) = Q(snap_t(R)) per operator.\nTHE-005 The event log is hash-chained; theia audit verify proves\nintegrity end-to-end. verify: chain-verification test incl. a\ndeliberately-corrupted fixture.\nThe gateway seam\nTHE-010 ALL model and embedding traffic crosses the gateway; no engine\nimports a provider SDK. verify: dependency lint; grep for provider imports\noutside gateway / harness-* / embeddings-*.\nTHE-011 Adapters are translational only — no retries, budgets, winner\nselection, or policy inside a harness adapter; callers own time. verify:\nreview question; no-retry-loop lint on harness-*.\nTHE-012 Harnesses are not roles; routing (who) and strategy (how many)\nnever share a control. verify: schema separation; grep for harness-id\nconditionals in kernel logic.\nTHE-013 Explicit subscription auth never falls back to an API key; an\nunavailable harness fails loudly with a reason, never silently substitutes.\nverify: routing tests for the unavailable-harness story.\nTHE-014 Data-class masking executes at the gateway before bytes leave\nthe process; prohibited content blocks the call — with ONE named\nexception: a caller whose completeness contract demands it (today only\nthe intent indexer, via CompletionRequest.maskProhibited ) gets the\nprohibited span REPLACED by its placeholder and the call proceeds, masked\ncounts visible on the audit event — a blocked intent call is a permanent\nindex hole while the secret never leaves the process either way.\nverify: masking golden tests; canary story with planted markers; the\ngateway test pinning that the same prompt blocks WITHOUT the flag.\nTHE-015 Cost is locked per call at execution from the embedded price\ntable and never recomputed from history. verify: cost-report test across a\nsimulated price change.\nTHE-016 Refusals, truncations, and schema failures are typed results\nscored honestly — never silently retried on another model. verify: eval\nprotocol review; result-kind metrics exist.\nTHE-017 Version resolution fails loud: a requested release\n( --x-release / THEIA_X_RELEASE ) that is neither cached nor downloadable\nis an error naming the version, the cache path, and the fix — the running\nbinary never silently substitutes itself; the dev tree never silently\nsubstitutes a release. verify: launcher resolution tests incl. the\nuncached+no-pat golden negative; hop-guard test.\nKernel & state\nTHE-020 One coordinator owns all run state; pools are stateless; agents\nmutate state only via typed verdicts and validated ∆Σ patches. verify:\nkernel-sim suite; no shared-state imports in pool code.\nTHE-021 Phase transitions live in one verified table; every status\nwrite routes through the guard; invariant breach freezes, never\nbest-effort-continues. verify: FSM walk test; grep for raw status writes.\nTHE-022 Σ-merge is the proved ⊕: deterministic, idempotent, convergent\nfor key-disjoint concurrent patches. verify: proofs/state_merge.v in CI;\nproperty tests mirroring the theorems.\nTHE-023 Plans mutate only through Π: executed nodes are monotone,\nevidence is never recomputed, only the ready frontier dispatches. verify:\nplan-algebra unit suite.\nTHE-024 Every loop is bounded: round budgets, no-progress guard,\ncost/wall caps — all policy values. verify: sim stories for each guard\nfiring.\nTHE-025 Delegation depth and fan-out are policy-bounded; every child is\na tracked task (no zombies). verify: delegation-chain sim story hitting the\nbound.\nTHE-026 A verifier's executed result is ground truth: it overrides any\nagent claim, and a verdict without cited execution evidence is invalid.\nverify: schema requires evidence refs; kernel test for claim-override.\nTHE-027 Fresh context per task: no role inherits another task's\ntranscript; cross-task knowledge flows only through the ledger state and\nmemory. verify: prompt-assembly test (inputs enumerable).\nCodebase model & memory\nTHE-030 Index entries are mutually independent and hash-keyed; a\nchanged file regenerates exactly its entries. verify: incremental-build\ntest counting model calls.\nTHE-031 Memory writes two substrates; canonical facts carry source-time\nand event-time separately; newer states never overwrite older evidence.\nverify: residence-style temporal fixture; schema NOT NULL on both times.\nTHE-032 Superseded versions are excluded from every recall path;\nversion chains keep history. verify: recall test over a superseded fixture.\nTHE-033 Structure is routing, never evidence: only leaf content enters\nanswer blocks. verify: block-assembly test asserting no summary-node text.\nTHE-034 Retrieval confidentiality is default-deny; caller filters can\nnarrow, never widen. verify: filter-override test.\nEvaluation & audit\nTHE-040 Contractual gates are mechanical; LLM judges appear only in\nreporting tiers. verify: gate-config lint (no judge in a gate).\nTHE-041 Benchmarks run production engine code; only the gateway backend\nswaps. verify: import graph of the bench adapters.\nTHE-042 Every default-on mechanism has a ledger row with a receipt;\nmeasured negatives are deleted and recorded. verify: ledger-coverage check\nagainst the gate/config registry.\nTHE-043 Replay equality holds for every recorded run. verify: CI replay\nof a pinned corpus of runs.\nTHE-044 Checker code is itself tested with golden positives AND golden\nnegatives. verify: meta-test convention; review question.\nSurfaces & schema\nTHE-050 Data shapes live in packages/schema and change there first;\nno surface forks a contract. verify: schema-gen diff gate.\nTHE-051 A field, knob, or policy value ships only with a real producer\nand consumer in the same change; dead knobs are deleted. verify:\nstaged-field check; knip.\nTHE-052 Surfaces are thin honest views: no app-only business logic, no\nfake states; every async surface can show loading/loaded/empty/failed;\nunknown ≠ zero, absence ≠ empty. verify: review question; DTO tests.\nTHE-053 Prompts live in.theia/prompts/ + baked defaults — never\ninline at call sites; enum lists in prompts interpolate from code enums.\nverify: prompt-drift test; grep for template literals with role text.\nTHE-054 The kernel is incapable of protected-branch merges and\nproduction actions — the capability is absent, not forbidden. verify: tool\ngrant catalog; canary attempting the action fails at dispatch.\nTHE-055 No PII and no model-authored text in telemetry, metrics, or\nexception messages; payloads travel as digests. verify: boundary denylist\ntests; sanitizer allowlist test.\nRelease & distribution\nTHE-060 No artifact reaches customers unverified: a release exists\nonly by promotion of a pre-release whose PUBLISHED artifacts passed the\ncustomer-shaped verification (public installer, pinned version,\nharness-fake functional smoke) on every supported target; promotion\nre-verifies the released bytes and retracts automatically on failure.\nverify: release.yml verify/verified jobs; promote.yml provenance check +\npost-verify + rollback.\nTHE-061 Pre-release versions carry the -pre.N suffix end to end —\ntag, artifact names, baked version — so no unsuffixed request can ever\nresolve one: latest excludes them by API contract, and an explicit\nselection must type the exact suffix. Customer releases bake the bare\nversion. verify: release.yml guard regex; launcher artifact-name tests;\ninstaller THEIA_VERSION path.\nTHE-062 The end-user update path resolves releases only; prereleases\nreach a machine solely via the explicit update.channel: prerelease opt-in (never written by the installer) or an exact suffixed version.\nverify: channel resolution tests; installer latest path.\nTHE-063 The installer is the recovery lever: re-running it replaces\nan existing install wholesale (stage-then-swap), verifies the binary RUNS\nbefore the PATH symlink flips, and purges the installed version from the\nlauncher cache — a broken release or corrupt cached auto-update is\nescaped by THEIA_VERSION=<good> install.sh, never by hand-surgery.\nverify: installer reinstall receipt; pre-flip run check in install.sh.\nTHE-064 Retention never orphans support: the newest two releases,\nthe newest pre-release, and every release-pins.yaml pin (version +\ncommit + reason + customer) survive every prune; deletion is dry-run\nfirst, destructive only under --apply. verify: prunePlan tests; the\npromote prune step prints the dry-run before applying.\nTHE-065 Vendored deliverables are the ONLY prompt source and are\nhash-verified end to end: no compiled prompt copy exists in code;\npackaged under the vendor tree's git hash with per-file sha256s; every\nrelease carries ITS OWN manifest embedded in the artifact, so\nverification and release-correspondence resolve OFFLINE — a pruned\nrelease still answers for itself; the update probe consults the RUNNING\nversion's release (manifest-only when treeHash matches), and a\nbroken/unresolvable vendor tree FAILS kernel verbs loudly while the\nrepair verbs (version/setup/self/doctor/init) stay runnable. verify:\nvendor.test.ts (coverage + doctor report + verification + precedence);\ndoctor vendor line; updateVendor pruned-release arm.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/demo.html":{"title":"Aletheia — the guided walkthrough","content":"A ~20-minute guided demo from first install to a completed, audited,\nreplayable agent run — the artifact we show CTOs, CEOs, analysts, and\nengineers, and the bar we measure feature-completeness against. Every\ncommand below is executable as printed; every number is a receipt from a\nreal execution against the reproducible demo workspace (regenerate it any\ntime — §3), not a projection. The TUI frames are captured text, not\nmockups. Deep references as we pass them: configuration\n( 15-configuration.md ), the TUI\n( 14-tui.md ), distribution\n( 16-release.md ), receipts\n( ledger.md ).\n1. Install (2 min)\nOne line, any Linux/macOS box, x86_64 or aarch64:\n$ curl -fsSL https://dokimelabs.github.io/get-theia/install.sh | sh\nGitHub PAT for dokimelabs/theia-releases (input hidden): ▌\nresolving latest release of dokimelabs/theia-releases…\nlatest: v0.1.0 → theia-v0.1.0-linux-x86_64.tar.gz\ndownloading theia-v0.1.0-linux-x86_64.tar.gz…\nsha256 verified\ninstalled theia v0.1.0 → ~/.local/share/theia/releases/0.1.0\nsymlinked ~/.local/bin/theia\nwrote ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (mode 0600) done — try: theia version && theia doctor\nTalking points: the installer detects os/arch and refuses unsupported\nplatforms by name (Windows is a planned first-class target, and it says\nso); the PAT is the access gate — releases live in a private repository,\nso distribution is licensing; the checksum verification is mandatory, not\nbest-effort; the config lands with 0600 permissions and the token is never\nechoed anywhere again.\nVersion discipline for the engineers in the room:\n$ theia version # the embedded release version\n$ theia --x-release 0.0.9 version # run EXACTLY that version (cached or # downloaded; missing+undownloadable = a # loud error, never a silent fallback)\n$ THEIA_X_RELEASE=0.0.9 theia … # the same, for scripts and CI\n$ theia --dev ~/work/theia-dev … # a developer runs the source tree\n$ theia self update # explicit; nothing updates itself\n2. Out of the box, and where everything is configured (3 min)\n$ mkdir pilot && cd pilot && theia doctor\ncodex subscription: ok\njail (bwrap): ok\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — ready at ~/.cache/theia/models/Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\nuser config: present\ngithub pat: present.theia store: not initialized (theia init)\nai rules: none detected\ncodex hooks: feature stable/true; project NOT trusted (gate 1); hooks.json absent (theia hooks install codex)\nDoctor probes — it runs real checks against real surfaces, so an\nunauthenticated harness is a visible failure at minute zero, never a\nmid-run surprise.\nThe out-of-the-box experience is deliberate: a bare theia init runs the config of record — every default was measured into place and carries a\nledger receipt; there is nothing to tune before the first run. When you do\nconfigure, everything lives in three visible places, all plain files under\nversion control:\nWhat Where Shown in this demo\nGovernance knobs (budgets, sandbox tier, routing, gates)\n.theia/config/*.yaml — k8s-shaped manifests, strict validation\n§4\nRole prompts\n.theia/prompts/ROLE.md — appended to baked prompts, digest-recorded per job\n§4\nThe user's own AI rules\ndetected at init; non-native files auto-wired into an audited Context manifest\n§3\nAn unknown field or a knob with no consumer fails loudly at load — a\nsetting that does nothing cannot exist. And if the team already has AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, or.cursor/rules, init wires them in\nautomatically: native files stay with the harness's own read, everything\nelse is injected as budgeted, digest-audited prompt content — \"what rules\ndid this run see\" is an event, not a guess.\n3. The demo workspace, from one command (3 min)\nThe demo environment is itself a product mechanism — a reproducible\nworkspace we can spin up to verify changes, run evaluations, or hold this\nexact walkthrough:\n$ theia eval demo create --dir ~/demo-cs\n…\ndemo workspace ready: repo ~/demo-cs @ 523efd6225 (suite green) store ~/demo-cs/.theia/store.db index: 71 written, 0 failed, 71 model calls rate: 3.7 files/s, $0.1500 locked (264.9s) brief GetInfo() omits async trigger behaviours (PermitIfAsync and friends)…\nWhat just happened: a real C# codebase (the stateless state-machine\nlibrary at a pinned commit — a genuine SWE-bench-Live instance, not a toy),\nits full test suite verified green at base,.theia/ initialized, demo\npolicy and memory seeded, and the intent index built:\n$ theia index build # again — the hash gate\nindex: 0 written, 71 current, 0 failed, 0 model calls\nrate: 71 reused (100%), 0 model calls, $0.0000 locked (10ms) $ theia index search \"GetInfo async trigger behaviours\"\n2.0164 [bm25+symbol] src/Stateless/StateMachine.cs — Provides the core finite-state-machine runtime for configuring states, firing triggers, executing transitions, and exposing machine metadata.\n1.0164 [bm25] src/Stateless/InternalTriggerBehaviour.cs — Provides guarded internal transition-action dispatch for synchronous and asynchronous state-machine triggers.\nTalking points: the index is the repository's intent layer — one entry\nper file carrying the design decisions no grep can see — built once\n(~$0.15 for ~70 files), then hash-gated forever: the second build costs\nzero model calls; editing one file re-indexes one file. Search unions\nlexical, vector, and AST-symbol lanes, consensus-first. The same map serves\nthe agents (§4), your Claude Code/Codex sessions (via MCP + hooks), and theia ask.\n4. The run — live following on the flight deck (7 min)\nThe task derives from the instance's real issue text (it is in DEMO.md in the workspace). Full-spec track first — plan, approve, execute:\n$ theia plan --task \"@TASKS/issue.md\" # the REAL upstream issue text [blueprint] src/Stateless/StateConfiguration.cs[engine/state-machine/9]: … [memory:fact:lexical-scan+recency] dotnet test is the verification gate … trace-metadata [fast_path] ✓ rg -n \"GetInfo|StateMachineInfo|…\" src tests. Inspect StateMachine.GetInfo(), StateRepresentation trigger-behaviour… add-regression-test [fast_path] ⇐ trace-metadata ✓ dotnet test Add a regression test configuring PermitIfAsync from state A to B… fix-info-aggregation [fast_path] ⇐ add-regression-test ✓ dotnet test Update the metadata aggregation path so GetInfo() includes async… validate-suite [fast_path] ⇐ fix-info-aggregation ✓ dotnet test Run the complete xUnit suite and review the resulting diff…\nverify pre-flight: `rg -n \"GetInfo|…\" src tests.` exit 2 at base — expected for red→green; if this is infrastructure, fix the command before approving\nverify pre-flight: `dotnet test` exit 1 at base — expected for red→green; if this is infrastructure (missing tool/framework), fix the command before approving\n(The pre-flight lines fire because the demo posture sets run.planVerifyPreflight: always — the knob is opt-in platform-wide, 15-configuration.md §3.)\nWorth thirty seconds of the room's attention: the planner read the map and\nthe seeded memory (both cited with provenance in its context block),\ndecomposed the issue into a dependency-ordered DAG — investigation first,\nregression tests, the fix, the full-suite check last — and authored the\nper-task verification commands itself.\nAnd then the platform checked the planner's homework. Every authored verify\ncommand just ran once at base — an observation, never a gate — and both\nflagged. plan show carries the labeled evidence:\n$ theia plan show pln2ak7y6nmlc…\n…\nverify pre-flight at base: ✖ exit 2 [root] rg -n \"GetInfo|StateMachineInfo|PermitIfAsync|…\" src tests. stderr: rg: tests: No such file or directory (os error 2) ✖ exit 1 [root] dotnet test stdout: … Passed: 422, Skipped: 0, Total: 423 … - Stateless.Tests.dll (net9.0) stderr: … Test Run Aborted.\nRead the second one closely — this is the demo's sharpest moment. The suite\nPASSES on net8.0/net9.0 (stdout) while the run as a whole ABORTS (stderr):\nthis repo multi-targets net8/net9/ net48, and the net48 test host demands\nMono. A CI pipeline wired with that command would flake forever; here the\nbroken check is visible before approval, with both streams labeled,\ninstead of rounds into a run. So teach the workspace — once:\n$ theia mem add constraint \"This repo multi-targets net8.0/net9.0/net48: \\ plain 'dotnet test' aborts (net48 host needs Mono) — every verify \\ command must pin '-f net8.0'. Tests live under test/ (singular)…\"\nadded fct2ak7y6w5v… $ theia mem add decision \"DAG plans gate each task's merge on its verify \\ going green, so a behavior change and its regression tests belong in \\ ONE task: test red, fix, merge green together. Never author a task \\ whose only work is running tests - attach the full-suite command as a \\ second verify entry on the fixing task (verify accepts a list).\"\nadded fct2ak7ypipb… $ theia plan --task \"@TASKS/issue.md\" # re-plan; memory now knows [memory:fact:lexical-scan+recency] Never author a plan task whose only work… [memory:fact:lexical-scan+recency] DAG plans gate each task's merge on its… [memory:fact:recency] This repo multi-targets net8.0/net9.0/net48: plain … include-async-trigger-info [fast_path] ✓ dotnet test test/Stateless.Tests/… -f net8.0 && dotnet test -f net8.0 Add regression coverage for GetInfo() with PermitIfAsync-configured transitions, then update the metadata construction path in StateMachine/StateMachineInfo so asynchronous trigger behaviours are included alongside synchronous behaviours without executing async guards. Verify the generated graph contains the A-to-B transition and existing metadata behavior remains unchanged.\nverify pre-flight: `dotnet test test/Stateless.Tests/… -f net8.0` passes at base\nverify pre-flight: `dotnet test -f net8.0` passes at base\ncost estimate: ~$0.09 (1 tasks × $0.094 avg/task from recorded runs) $ theia plan approve pln2ak7yprsmw…\napproved pln2ak7yprsmw @ …\n$ theia run --from-plan pln2ak7yprsmw… --record --tui\nTwo teachings, and every future plan in this workspace authors runnable\nchecks and a convergent shape: ONE task carrying the whole red→green arc\nwith an ordered LIST of verify commands — the focused suite, then the full\nsuite as the regression guard — both cited right in the planner's context\nblock. (Had a broken command slipped through anyway, the run is still not\ndoomed: the lead can rewrite a task's verify commands on a rescope, audited\non the verdict event — the same failure class has an in-run lever, and its\nrescope note reaches the next implementer as binding direction. The\nledger's §4 carries the full sagas of these guards earning their keep on\nthis very workspace.)\nThe plan is a content-hashed artifact: run --from-plan refuses\nanything but approved, any edit is a new digest back in draft, and every\ntask records the digest it satisfies — the SPEC_APPROVED gate as a\nmechanism, not a checklist row.\nOn an interactive terminal the run opens the run tree — live:\n⢿ running · run run2ak7ypvyog222vtzpgsqut · fast_path · $0.0446/$5.00 · 1 call · 25 events 3m57s\ntasks: 1 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 [@ event 25/25 · live]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ⢿ ▼ tsk…t2ouhe25 [fast_path] review r2 $0.0446 3m57s ✔ ├╴r1 implement implementer → ready 7 evidence 3m15s ✔ ├╴r2 verify verifier → verify_pass 2 evidence 15s ⢿ ╰╴r3 review … 28s ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow:round • e err • m steer •? • q stop\nReading the frame: the header carries the full run id, track, the cost\nticker against budget, and a worded task tally; each task is a collapsible\nnode with per-round children; the braille spinner marks what is in flight\nNOW; durations tick on the right. Dagger's law: success condenses,\nfailure flags itself — merged work collapses to one ✔ row; anything ✖ / ◆ auto-expands and e jumps to it.\nThree live verbs to show:\nSteer — press m, type \"prefer the smallest fix; do not refactor\",\nEnter. The note lands in the hash-chained log AND the live\ncoordinator's guidance — the next dispatch carries it. Steering is\naudited and replayable, and with the cursor on a task node it targets\nthat task alone.\nVerbosity — press + twice:\n✔ completed · run run2ak7ypvyog222vtzpgsqut · fast_path · $0.2472/$5.00 · 6 calls · 81 events\ntasks: 0 running · 1 done · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠3 [@ event 81/81 · head]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ✔ ▼ tsk…t2ouhe25 [fast_path] merged r9 $0.2472 9m22s · ├╴[include-async-trigger-info] Add regression coverage for GetInfo() with PermitIfAsync-configur… ✔ ├╴workspace @ 523efd6 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready 7 evidence 3m15s ✔ ├╴▶ r2 verify verifier → verify_pass 2 evidence 15s ✔ ├╴▶ r3 review reviewer → rework 3 evidence 50s ✔ ├╴▶ r4 arbitrate arbiter → arb_escalate 5 evidence 40s ✔ ├╴▶ r5 escalated lead → lead_rescope 5 evidence 56s ✔ ├╴▼ r6 implement implementer → ready 6 evidence 2m20s ✔ │ ├╴▼ · gateway codex-write ok $0.0274 kind: ok masked: {} model: null priceSource: embedded ✔ │ │ ├╴$ sed -n '1,240p' src/Stateless/StateMachine.cs && sed -n '1,240p' src/Stateless/Reflectio… argsShort: sed -n '1,240p' src/Stateless/StateMachine.cs && sed -n '1,240p' src/Stateles… blobRef: 1b4b5340cfe4ff7c75b54e44442aeb47d5d5898f37e9b332269340570e82a7e7 callKey: 1b83885f3e50e4835cdc93997b966db54be73630100e6aea28d0e6222f18f147 durationMs: null ✔ │ │ ├╴$ rg -n \\\"PermitIfAsync|TransitioningTriggerBehaviourAsync|GetInfo_should|FixedTransitions… argsShort: rg -n \\\"PermitIfAsync|TransitioningTriggerBehaviourAsync|GetInfo_should|Fixed… blobRef: b05686893b47e9d311e5e65611bd1aa9155aa2ed53e925c0c56a8f9d63823786 callKey: 1b83885f3e50e4835cdc93997b966db54be73630100e6aea28d0e6222f18f147 durationMs: null ✖ │ │ ├╴$ sed -n '100,175p' test/Stateless.Tests/ReflectionFixture.cs rg -n \\\"class TransitioningT… argsShort: sed -n '100,175p' test/Stateless.Tests/ReflectionFixture.cs rg -n \\\"class Tra… ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • +/- verbosity=2 •? • q quit\nThe tree reveals what lives under a round — the gateway call with its\nLOCKED cost and price source, and every command the agent ran, each with\na content-addressed blob ref; v opens any full command/output in $EDITOR from the local blob store. (This frame browses the finished\nrun — the identical fold renders live, rows appearing as the agent makes\nthem.) Read the round list above it: the reviewer BOUNCED the first pass\n(r3 rework — the graph regression was not actually asserted), the\narbiter escalated, the lead rescoped with concrete direction, and r6's\nimplementer acted on it to approval — the review loop earning its keep\ninside a nine-round merge. Press - back to the executive altitude —\nverbosity 0 condenses to task headers (anomalies and steering always\nstay visible: honesty is not a tier).\n3. Stop/resume (mention, don't perform): q once = graceful pause,\nin-flight verdicts land, theia run --resume continues from the log; a\nhard kill costs only in-flight attempts — the log is always a\nchain-intact prefix.\nWhile it runs, the trust story: the implementer works in an isolated\nworkspace copy; agents never run git — the platform commits, and only\nthe merger writes trunk (push capability does not exist anywhere in the\nkernel); the verifier executes the actual test suite in the task's own\nworkspace and its exit code overrides any model claim — the \"agents\ngrading their own homework\" question has a mechanical answer.\nCompletion:\nrun run2ak7ypvyog222vtzpgsqut plan pln2ak7yprsmw223tk2wxg3k3 @ 8817582fe4c6… → 1 tasks\ntsk2ak7ypvyzg222vt2ouhe25 → merged (9 rounds) [include-async-trigger-info] Add regression coverage f\nor Ge…\nhalted: completed · 6 gateway calls · $0.2472\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 4.752821239999999\nmaintenance: 0 summaries refreshed, 0 orphan generations GCed\n5. After the run — audit, time travel, counterfactuals (3 min)\n$ theia show <runId> --tui # browse the stored run\n⣷ running · run run2ak7ypvyog222vtzpgsqut · fast_path · $0.1776/$5.00 · 5 calls · 73 events\ntasks: 1 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠3 [@ event 73/81]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ⣷ ▼ tsk…t2ouhe25 [fast_path] review r7 $0.1776 9m22s · ├╴[include-async-trigger-info] Add regression coverage for GetInfo() with PermitIfAsync-configur… ✔ ├╴workspace @ 523efd6 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready 7 evidence 3m15s ✔ ├╴▶ r2 verify verifier → verify_pass 2 evidence 15s ✔ ├╴▶ r3 review reviewer → rework 3 evidence 50s ✔ ├╴▶ r4 arbitrate arbiter → arb_escalate 5 evidence 40s ✔ ├╴▶ r5 escalated lead → lead_rescope 5 evidence 56s ✔ ├╴▶ r6 implement implementer → ready 6 evidence 2m20s ✔ ├╴▶ r7 verify verifier → verify_pass 2 evidence 14s ⣷ ╰╴▼ r8 review … 52s ✔ ├╴$ git status --short && git diff -- src/Stateless/StateMachine.cs src/Stateless/Reflection/S… argsShort: git status --short && git diff -- src/Stateless/StateMachine.cs src/Stateless/R… blobRef: 29e541f5e82750a26df11cca915f1121506671679e9c3545a34d47f636fffa74 callKey: a045e72e17e476edcc191dd0b5644944dd8fd0352b2eec679c2a815674dab2b2 durationMs: null ✔ ├╴$ git status --short && git log -1 --oneline && sed -n '35,80p' test/Stateless.Tests/GetInfo… argsShort: git status --short && git log -1 --oneline && sed -n '35,80p' test/Stateless.Te… blobRef: d7ea9764e3651a3fec62404976e48375d7ed0c616fba1b854b677293dc1e7032 callKey: a045e72e17e476edcc191dd0b5644944dd8fd0352b2eec679c2a815674dab2b2 durationMs: null ✔ ├╴$ sed -n '1,220p' src/Stateless/Reflection/FixedTransitionInfo.cs && sed -n '1,180p' src/Sta… argsShort: sed -n '1,220p' src/Stateless/Reflection/FixedTransitionInfo.cs && sed -n '1,18… blobRef: 93f5566fdd477a84d17002faf8e796f937ac45a59f14d0677c2c814c27334fdb callKey: a045e72e17e476edcc191dd0b5644944dd8fd0352b2eec679c2a815674dab2b2 durationMs: null ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • +/- verbosity=2 •? • q quit\nScrub time with [ and ] — the tree re-folds from the event prefix:\n\"what did the run look like the moment the verifier passed\" is two\nkeystrokes. Every verdict node carries a copyable theia fork --at command — fork the run at that decision with different steering, and theia compare reports divergence, cost delta, and outcome delta, with\nthe shared prefix replayed from recordings for free.\n$ theia replay <runId> # byte-identical re-fold; the determinism gate\n$ theia audit verify # every hash chain, end to end\n$ theia probe <runId> \"why did the implementer touch only one file?\"\n$ theia ask \"what is the verification gate in this workspace?\"\nThe verification gate is `dotnet test`; the xUnit suite must remain green.\n[fct:2ak7xr76o6…] [sources] fct:2ak7xr76o6… chk:2ak7xr76oc… src/Stateless/… · run:2ak7xtth3s…\n[ask run2ak7xuaad2… — $0.0010]\nask and probe answer with citations and abstain at $0 when the\nrecords do not support an answer — an unsupported question cannot\nhallucinate. If anything ever goes wrong instead, theia diagnose <runId> runs root-cause analysis over the audit trail alone and remembers the\nfailure fingerprint — the platform has diagnosed its own integration gaps\nthis way (three shipped fixes carry those receipts in the ledger).\n6. The close (2 min)\nEvery claim is an artifact. 608 tests, 2 machine-checked\nproofs, a hash-chained audit log per run, replay equality as an\ninvariant, and a methods ledger where every default carries its receipt\n— including the honest negatives.\nGovernance is mechanical. The playbook rows a regulated buyer asks\nfor — spec approval, stop-and-fix, human merge authority, data-class\nmasking before any byte leaves the process, tamper-evident logs,\nagent_run_id-grade attribution — each exists here as a mechanism this\ndemo just poked ( 00-vision.md §4 ).\nThe cost story is honest. Every model call locks its USD at\nexecution; budgets pause resumable, never explode; the index amortizes\nto zero on unchanged code; and the evaluation harness measures the\nplatform against raw single-model baselines on live benchmarks — the\ndeltas are published per difficulty bucket, not as one aggregate\n( notes/06-eval-plan.md ).\nDeferred work is named and scoped in 13-roadmap.md — queue rows, not gaps.","keywords":[],"tags":[]}}